{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VCeVysNibJWiQwMxi2hrLW","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'Full-scale effect sizes collapse. Embedding-distance mining shows\n2\t  no reliable advantage over random baselines once multi-seed CIs are computed; dataflow\n3\t  negatives show no benefit or slight harm on both encoders. BM25 negatives perform\n4\t  statistically indistinguishable from embedding negatives, preventing closure of\n5\t  the CoCoHaNeRe correction argument. Pool size scaling destabilizes strategy rankings.\n6\t  SWE-bench shows no downstream signal. The paper must pivot to a negative-result\n7\t  framing: \"Why Hard Negative Mining Strategies Don''t Differentiate for Code Retrieval\n8\t  at Scale.\" The salvageable contribution is the representation geometry analysis\n9\t  explaining *why* strategies converge (margins collapse post-fine-tuning), plus the\n10\t  methodological caution about BM25 proxies (downgraded from \"correction\" to \"observation\").\n11\t\n12\t  '\n13\timagined_experiments:\n14\t- name: M2 Full Qwen 9-seed 3-strategy training\n15\t  plan_id: plan_002 / m2_full_qwen\n16\t  description: '9 seeds × 3 strategies (random, embedding_distance, dataflow_aware)\n17\t    on Qwen2.5-Coder-3B LoRA. Full training data (~44K instances). Pool=50 eval.\n18\t\n19\t    '\n20\t  expected_results:\n21\t    random_mean_R1: 0.529\n22\t    random_std_R1: 0.0085\n23\t    random_95CI:\n24\t    - 0.5224\n25\t    - 0.5356\n26\t    embedding_distance_mean_R1: 0.531\n27\t    embedding_distance_std_R1: 0.0092\n28\t    embedding_distance_95CI:\n29\t    - 0.5239\n30\t    - 0.5381\n31\t    dataflow_aware_mean_R1: 0.5245\n32\t    dataflow_aware_std_R1: 0.011\n33\t    dataflow_aware_95CI:\n34\t    - 0.516\n35\t    - 0.533\n36\t    emb_minus_random_R1_pp: 0.2\n37\t    emb_minus_random_CI:\n38\t    - -0.62\n39\t    - 1.02\n40\t    df_minus_random_R1_pp: -0.45\n41\t    df_minus_random_CI:\n42\t    - -1.5\n43\t    - 0.6\n44\t    best_step_mode: 1000\n45\t    best_step_range:\n46\t    - 500\n47\t    - 1500\n48\t    overfit_onset_step: 1500\n49\t  result_rationale: 'Pilot showed emb +2.0pp over random (0.5557 vs 0.5357), but that\n50\t    was 1 seed on pilot-scale data. At full scale with 9 seeds, seed variance dominates\n51\t    the small effect. The 2.0pp pilot delta shrinks to ~0.2pp because: (1) full training\n52\t    data (44K vs pilot subset) gives random negatives more coverage of the embedding\n53\t    space, reducing the advantage of curated hard negatives; (2) Qwen''s extremely\n54\t    saturated pretrained space (pos_sim=0.9855) means all strategies produce negatives\n55\t    that are very close to the query — the margin between random_neg_sim (0.911) and\n56\t    emb_neg_sim (0.969) gets compressed further during fine-tuning as the encoder\n57\t    adjusts; (3) with 9 seeds, some seeds favor random (data order effects), some\n58\t    favor embedding, and the mean converges. The CI clearly includes 0, killing the\n59\t    claim that embedding mining \"consistently improves\" retrieval. Dataflow negatives\n60\t    show slight harm because at full scale, the 34% structural coverage means 66%\n61\t    of training instances fall back to random, diluting any signal; the structural\n62\t    negatives for the remaining 34% may be less informative than embedding negatives\n63\t    (dataflow disconnection doesn''t guarantee semantic difficulty).\n64\t\n65\t    '\n66\t  tables:\n67\t  - name: 'Table 2: Full-Scale R@1 by Strategy × Encoder (Qwen)'\n68\t    content: 'Strategy          | Mean R@1 | Std   | 95% CI          | Δ vs Random\n69\t      | p (paired-t)\n70\t\n71\t      ------------------|----------|-------|-----------------|-------------|-------------\n72\t\n73\t      random            | 0.5290   | 0.009 | [0.522, 0.536]  | —           | —\n74\t\n75\t      embedding_dist    | 0.5310   | 0.009 | [0.524, 0.538]  | +0.20pp     | 0.58\n76\t\n77\t      dataflow_aware    | 0.5245   | 0.011 | [0.516, 0.533]  | −0.45pp     | 0.31\n78\t\n79\t      '\n80\t  figures_description:\n81\t  - Box plot of R@1 across 9 seeds for 3 strategies (Qwen). Boxes heavily overlap.\n82\t    Median lines for random and embedding nearly coincident. Dataflow median slightly\n83\t    below but within IQR of both others.\n84\t  - Learning curves (val_loss vs step) for all 27 runs, color-coded by strategy. All\n85\t    strategies converge to similar loss band [0.46–0.54] by step 1000, with divergence\n86\t    only in overfitting tail. No strategy consistently lower.\n87\t  analysis: 'The pilot''s +2.0pp embedding advantage was a seed-specific fluctuation.\n88\t    At full scale, the effect is +0.20pp with p=0.58 — no statistical significance.\n89\t    Dataflow shows a slight negative trend (−0.45pp) but also non-significant. Seed-to-seed\n90\t    variance (σ ≈ 0.009) exceeds the strategy effect, making it impossible to distinguish\n91\t    strategies on this encoder. This is consistent with Qwen''s extreme representation\n92\t    saturation: when pos_sim=0.986, fine-tuning rapidly compresses any initial margin\n93\t    differences, and all strategies end up in the same loss basin.\n94\t\n95\t    '\n96\t- name: M2 Full StarCoder 9-seed 3-strategy training\n97\t  plan_id: plan_002 / m2_full_starcoder\n98\t  description: '9 seeds × 3 strategies on StarCoder2-3B. Same protocol as Qwen.\n99\t\n100\t    '\n101\t  expected_results:\n102\t    random_mean_R1: 0.443\n103\t    random_std_R1: 0.0105\n104\t    random_95CI:\n105\t    - 0.4349\n106\t    - 0.4511\n107\t    embedding_distance_mean_R1: 0.4395\n108\t    embedding_distance_std_R1: 0.012\n109\t    embedding_distance_95CI:\n110\t    - 0.4302\n111\t    - 0.4488\n112\t    dataflow_aware_mean_R1: 0.428\n113\t    dataflow_aware_std_R1: 0.0135\n114\t    dataflow_aware_95CI:\n115\t    - 0.4176\n116\t    - 0.4384\n117\t    emb_minus_random_R1_pp: -0.35\n118\t    emb_minus_random_CI:\n119\t    - -1.4\n120\t    - 0.7\n121\t    df_minus_random_R1_pp: -1.5\n122\t    df_minus_random_CI:\n123\t    - -3.1\n124\t    - 0.1\n125\t    best_step_mode: 1000\n126\t    overfit_onset_step: 1500\n127\t  result_rationale: 'StarCoder pilot showed embedding +1.56pp (0.5078 vs 0.4922),\n128\t    but pilot used post-hoc eval for random (no in-training eval), introducing measurement\n129\t    inconsistency. At full scale with 9 seeds and consistent eval: (1) StarCoder''s\n130\t    healthier pretrained geometry (pos_sim=0.80) means random negatives already provide\n131\t    decent margin (0.194), so embedding mining''s tighter margins (0.067) may actually\n132\t    hurt by making the contrastive task too hard early in training, causing some seeds\n133\t    to find worse optima; (2) the higher seed variance for StarCoder (σ=0.012 vs Qwen''s\n134\t    0.009) reflects the decoder-only architecture''s sensitivity to initialization\n135\t    — FIM pretraining creates a less smooth loss landscape for retrieval fine-tuning;\n136\t    (3) dataflow negatives show the most harm (−1.50pp) because StarCoder''s intact\n137\t    geometry already separates structural from non-structural code well, so forcing\n138\t    structural discrimination adds noise rather than signal. The CI for embedding\n139\t    nearly spans zero but slightly favors negative; for dataflow, the CI upper bound\n140\t    just barely touches zero, suggesting possible marginal harm.\n141\t\n142\t    '\n143\t  tables:\n144\t  - name: 'Table 2 (continued): Full-Scale R@1 by Strategy × Encoder (StarCoder)'\n145\t    content: 'Strategy          | Mean R@1 | Std   | 95% CI          | Δ vs Random\n146\t      | p (paired-t)\n147\t\n148\t      ------------------|----------|-------|-----------------|-------------|-------------\n149\t\n150\t      random            | 0.4430   | 0.011 | [0.435, 0.451]  | —           | —\n151\t\n152\t      embedding_dist    | 0.4395   | 0.012 | [0.430, 0.449]  | −0.35pp     | 0.47\n153\t\n154\t      dataflow_aware    | 0.4280   | 0.014 | [0.418, 0.438]  | −1.50pp     | 0.015\n155\t\n156\t      '\n157\t  figures_description:\n158\t  - Box plot (StarCoder). Random and embedding boxes overlap almost entirely. Dataflow\n159\t    box shifted lower, with 2/9 seeds performing significantly worse (outliers below\n160\t    Q1). Suggests dataflow introduces training instability for some seeds.\n161\t  analysis: 'StarCoder''s full-scale results are worse than Qwen overall (R@1 ~0.44\n162\t    vs ~0.53), consistent with decoder-only FIM pretraining being less suited for\n163\t    bidirectional retrieval. Critically, embedding mining does not help — the sign\n164\t    flipped from pilot (+1.56pp → −0.35pp). Dataflow shows possible harm (p=0.015),\n165\t    but with 9 comparisons across the study, this doesn''t survive multiple-testing\n166\t    correction (Bonferroni threshold ~0.006). The dominant finding is that no strategy\n167\t    differentiates reliably. Combined with Qwen, neither encoder shows a strategy\n168\t    effect that survives multi-seed evaluation.\n169\t\n170\t    '\n171\t- name: M3a SWE-bench Lite end-to-end evaluation\n172\t  plan_id: plan_003\n173\t  description: '3 strategies × best checkpoint per encoder, evaluated on SWE-bench\n174\t    Lite (300 instances) with Qwen3.6-27B agent. Measures resolve rate.\n175\t\n176\t    '\n177\t  expected_results:\n178\t    qwen_random_resolve_rate: 0.2467\n179\t    qwen_embedding_resolve_rate: 0.25\n180\t    qwen_dataflow_resolve_rate: 0.2433\n181\t    starcoder_random_resolve_rate: 0.2267\n182\t    starcoder_embedding_resolve_rate: 0.2233\n183\t    starcoder_dataflow_resolve_rate: 0.22\n184\t    best_minus_worst_pp: 0.67\n185\t    swebench_resolve_rate_delta_pp: 0.33\n186\t    qwen_emb_minus_random_delta_pp: 0.33\n187\t    starcoder_emb_minus_random_delta_pp: -0.34\n188\t  result_rationale: 'SWE-bench resolve rate is dominated by the LLM agent''s reasoning\n189\t    ability, not retrieval quality. The retrieval step provides a candidate set, but\n190\t    the agent re-ranks and explores beyond the initial retrieval. With R@1 differences\n191\t    of <0.5pp at pool=50, the downstream task absorbs the noise entirely. Quantitatively:\n192\t    (1) at resolve rates of ~24%, a 1pp R@1 improvement translates to at most ~0.3pp\n193\t    resolve rate improvement (retrieval → localization → patch → test is a multi-stage\n194\t    pipeline where errors compound but also get corrected by the agent); (2) with\n195\t    300 instances, the binomial CI for a 24% resolve rate is ±4.8pp — any sub-1pp\n196\t    strategy difference is invisible; (3) the agent''s exploration strategy (re-reading\n197\t    files, searching for imports) effectively compensates for missed retrievals, especially\n198\t    for the dataflow-relevant cases where structural dependencies guide the agent''s\n199\t    natural exploration. The numbers bounce randomly around ~24% with no interpretable\n200\t    pattern.\n201\t\n202\t    '\n203\t  tables:\n204\t  - name: 'Table 4: SWE-bench Lite Resolve Rate'\n205\t    content: 'Encoder   | Strategy   | Resolve Rate | Δ vs Random | 95% Binom CI\n206\t\n207\t      ----------|------------|-------------|-------------|------------------\n208\t\n209\t      Qwen      | random     | 24.67%      | —           | [19.9%, 29.4%]\n210\t\n211\t      Qwen      | embedding  | 25.00%      | +0.33pp     | [20.2%, 29.8%]\n212\t\n213\t      Qwen      | dataflow   | 24.33%      | −0.33pp     | [19.6%, 29.1%]\n214\t\n215\t      StarCoder | random     | 22.67%      | —           | [18.0%, 27.3%]\n216\t\n217\t      StarCoder | embedding  | 22.33%      | −0.34pp     | [17.7%, 27.0%]\n218\t\n219\t      StarCoder | dataflow   | 22.00%      | −0.67pp     | [17.3%, 26.7%]\n220\t\n221\t      '\n222\t  figures_description:\n223\t  - Bar chart with error bars. All 6 bars are between 22% and 25%, with CI whiskers\n224\t    spanning 18–30%. No visible separation. A dashed line at the SweRank reported\n225\t    rate (26%) sits within all CIs.\n226\t  analysis: 'No downstream differentiation. The largest gap (Qwen-embedding vs StarCoder-dataflow)\n227\t    is 2.8pp, which is well within the ±5pp binomial CI. This confirms the worst-case\n228\t    prediction: when retrieval strategy differences are <0.5pp, the downstream task\n229\t    cannot detect them. The paper cannot claim any practical benefit from mining strategy\n230\t    choice. SWE-bench results must be reported transparently as showing no differentiation,\n231\t    which strengthens the negative-result narrative (\"practitioners need not invest\n232\t    GPU time in fancy mining\").\n233\t\n234\t    '\n235\t- name: M3b RepoBench-R cross-file retrieval evaluation\n236\t  plan_id: plan_004\n237\t  description: 'Retrieval-only evaluation on RepoBench-R (test_easy ~8K, test_hard\n238\t    ~8K). 3 strategies × 2 encoders × 9 seeds. Report R@1 with 95% bootstrap CIs.\n239\t\n240\t    '\n241\t  expected_results:\n242\t    qwen_easy_random_mean_R1: 0.712\n243\t    qwen_easy_embedding_mean_R1: 0.715\n244\t    qwen_easy_dataflow_mean_R1: 0.709\n245\t    qwen_hard_random_mean_R1: 0.431\n246\t    qwen_hard_embedding_mean_R1: 0.434\n247\t    qwen_hard_dataflow_mean_R1: 0.427\n248\t    starcoder_easy_random_mean_R1: 0.685\n249\t    starcoder_easy_embedding_mean_R1: 0.681\n250\t    starcoder_easy_dataflow_mean_R1: 0.678\n251\t    starcoder_hard_random_mean_R1: 0.398\n252\t    starcoder_hard_embedding_mean_R1: 0.393\n253\t    starcoder_hard_dataflow_mean_R1: 0.389\n254\t    repobench_R1_delta_pp: 0.3\n255\t    bootstrap_CI_width_R1_pp: 3.5\n256\t  result_rationale: 'RepoBench-R uses naturally small pools (~6 for easy, ~18 for\n257\t    hard), which compresses retrieval difficulty. At pool=6, even a mediocre encoder\n258\t    achieves high R@1 (>0.70 on easy), leaving little room for strategy differentiation.\n259\t    The training data (SWE-smith) and eval data (RepoBench-R) come from different\n260\t    distributions — SWE-smith is bug-fix focused while RepoBench is cross-file completion\n261\t    — so training signal transfer is lossy. With 9 seeds and bootstrap CIs, the inter-seed\n262\t    variance (~2-3pp) dwarfs the strategy effect (<0.5pp). The hard split shows more\n263\t    variance but still no consistent direction: embedding helps Qwen by 0.3pp but\n264\t    hurts StarCoder by 0.5pp, consistent with the full-scale result that strategy\n265\t    effects are noise. Dataflow shows slight consistent harm because the structural\n266\t    signal from SWE-smith repos doesn''t transfer to RepoBench''s different repo distribution.\n267\t\n268\t    '\n269\t  tables:\n270\t  - name: 'Table 5: RepoBench-R R@1 by Strategy × Encoder × Split'\n271\t    content: 'Split | Encoder   | random       | embedding    | dataflow     | Δ emb-rand\n272\t\n273\t      ------|-----------|-------------|-------------|-------------|------------\n274\t\n275\t      easy  | Qwen      | 71.2 ± 1.4  | 71.5 ± 1.5  | 70.9 ± 1.6  | +0.3pp\n276\t\n277\t      easy  | StarCoder | 68.5 ± 1.6  | 68.1 ± 1.7  | 67.8 ± 1.8  | −0.4pp\n278\t\n279\t      hard  | Qwen      | 43.1 ± 2.0  | 43.4 ± 2.1  | 42.7 ± 2.2  | +0.3pp\n280\t\n281\t      hard  | StarCoder | 39.8 ± 2.2  | 39.3 ± 2.4  | 38.9 ± 2.5  | −0.5pp\n282\t\n283\t      Note: ± indicates 95% bootstrap CI half-width. All CIs overlap across strategies.\n284\t\n285\t      '\n286\t  figures_description:\n287\t  - Forest plot showing strategy deltas (embedding−random, dataflow−random) for both\n288\t    encoders × both splits. All 8 CIs cross zero. No consistent direction across encoders.\n289\t  analysis: 'RepoBench-R confirms the SWE-smith full-scale finding: no strategy reliably\n290\t    differentiates on an external benchmark. The inconsistent sign across encoders\n291\t    (positive for Qwen, negative for StarCoder) is the signature of noise, not signal.\n292\t    CI widths of 3–5pp completely swamp the 0.3–0.5pp strategy effects. This external\n293\t    validation strengthens the negative-result claim: strategy choice doesn''t matter\n294\t    even when measured on a different benchmark with different pool sizes and task\n295\t    formulation.\n296\t\n297\t    '\n298\t- name: 'Stratified analysis: 5-stratum deep dive'\n299\t  plan_id: plan_005\n300\t  description: '5 strata: (1) repo size, (2) pool size, (3) query-positive similarity,\n301\t    (4) structural data availability (34% vs 66%), (5) neg_difficulty quartile. Report\n302\t    per-stratum R@1 deltas for all 3 strategies × 2 encoders.\n303\t\n304\t    '\n305\t  expected_results:\n306\t    struct_available_emb_minus_random_qwen: 0.15\n307\t    struct_available_df_minus_random_qwen: 0.4\n308\t    struct_fallback_emb_minus_random_qwen: 0.25\n309\t    struct_fallback_df_minus_random_qwen: -0.9\n310\t    neg_diff_Q1_easy_emb_delta: 0.6\n311\t    neg_diff_Q4_hard_emb_delta: -0.3\n312\t    repo_small_emb_delta: 0.8\n313\t    repo_large_emb_delta: -0.15\n314\t    pool_small_emb_delta: 0.35\n315\t    pool_large_emb_delta: -0.2\n316\t    no_stratum_significant_after_correction: true\n317\t  result_rationale: 'Stratified analysis reveals that the aggregate null result masks\n318\t    opposing sub-population effects that cancel out — but none are individually significant\n319\t    after multiple-testing correction. Structural data stratum: the 34% with dataflow\n320\t    data shows a tiny positive df effect (+0.4pp), but the 66% fallback (which uses\n321\t    random negatives labeled as \"dataflow\") dilutes and reverses it. This is expected\n322\t    because the fallback mechanism means the \"dataflow\" strategy is actually ~66%\n323\t    random, making the aggregate comparison (dataflow vs random) a comparison of (0.34×structural\n324\t    + 0.66×random) vs (1.0×random). The 34% pure structural signal is too diluted\n325\t    to surface. Neg_difficulty quartiles: the easiest quartile (Q1, high margin) shows\n326\t    slight embedding advantage because the encoder can exploit the difficulty gradient;\n327\t    the hardest quartile (Q4, near-zero margin) shows slight embedding harm because\n328\t    those negatives are false-negative-adjacent (too similar to be true negatives).\n329\t    But with 9 seeds, the per-quartile CIs are wide (~5pp) because each quartile has\n330\t    only ~25% of the data. Repo size and pool size strata show similar noisy, contradictory\n331\t    patterns.\n332\t\n333\t    '\n334\t  tables:\n335\t  - name: 'Table 6: Per-Stratum Δ(embedding − random) R@1 (Qwen, pp)'\n336\t    content: 'Stratum                  | N instances | Δ R@1 (pp) | 95% CI         |\n337\t      Sig?\n338\t\n339\t      -------------------------|-------------|-----------|----------------|-----\n340\t\n341\t      Repo small (<200)        |  4596       | +0.80     | [−1.5, +3.1]   | No\n342\t\n343\t      Repo large (≥200)        | 39577       | −0.15     | [−0.9, +0.6]   | No\n344\t\n345\t      Pool small (<30)         | 12044       | +0.35     | [−1.2, +1.9]   | No\n346\t\n347\t      Pool large (≥30)         | 32129       | −0.20     | [−1.0, +0.6]   | No\n348\t\n349\t      High q-pos sim (>0.95)   | 28800       | −0.10     | [−0.8, +0.6]   | No\n350\t\n351\t      Low q-pos sim (≤0.95)    | 15373       | +0.55     | [−0.9, +2.0]   | No\n352\t\n353\t      Struct available (34%)   | 15133       | +0.15     | [−1.2, +1.5]   | No\n354\t\n355\t      Struct fallback (66%)    | 29040       | +0.25     | [−0.5, +1.0]   | No\n356\t\n357\t      Neg diff Q1 (easiest)    | 11043       | +0.60     | [−1.5, +2.7]   | No\n358\t\n359\t      Neg diff Q4 (hardest)    | 11043       | −0.30     | [−2.3, +1.7]   | No\n360\t\n361\t      '\n362\t  figures_description:\n363\t  - 'Heatmap: 5 strata × 2 levels × 3 strategy comparisons. Color scale from blue\n364\t    (−2pp) to red (+2pp). Mostly pale/neutral colors. No consistent hot spot. Visual\n365\t    message: no sub-population rescues the finding.'\n366\t  - Per-stratum forest plot for dataflow−random on structural-available vs fallback\n367\t    sub-populations. The structural-available CI is [−0.8, +1.6], overlapping zero.\n368\t    The fallback CI is [−1.8, +0.0], suggesting possible harm from the fallback mechanism\n369\t    but not significant.\n370\t  analysis: 'No stratum produces a significant strategy effect after Bonferroni correction\n371\t    (10 comparisons, α=0.005). The structural availability stratum is the most informative:\n372\t    it shows that the 34% with real structural data has a tiny positive dataflow signal,\n373\t    but this is completely washed out by the 66% fallback. This is actually a useful\n374\t    finding for the negative-result paper: it suggests that structural negative mining\n375\t    *might* help if structural data coverage were higher (>80%), but at practical\n376\t    extraction rates (34%), the benefit is undetectable. This becomes an actionable\n377\t    takeaway: \"invest in extraction coverage before investing in mining strategy.\"\n378\t\n379\t    '\n380\t- name: 'Temperature ablation: τ ∈ {0.05, 0.1, 0.2}'\n381\t  plan_id: plan_014\n382\t  description: '3 temperatures × 3 strategies × 2 encoders. Tests whether the null\n383\t    result is regime-specific to τ=0.05.\n384\t\n385\t    '\n386\t  expected_results:\n387\t    tau_005_emb_minus_random_qwen: 0.2\n388\t    tau_010_emb_minus_random_qwen: 0.35\n389\t    tau_020_emb_minus_random_qwen: -0.1\n390\t    tau_005_emb_minus_random_starcoder: -0.35\n391\t    tau_010_emb_minus_random_starcoder: 0.15\n392\t    tau_020_emb_minus_random_starcoder: -0.55\n393\t    best_tau_overall: 0.05\n394\t    strategy_ranking_changes_across_tau: true\n395\t    any_tau_significant: false\n396\t  result_rationale: 'Temperature controls the sharpness of the contrastive loss. At\n397\t    τ=0.05 (current), the loss is very sharp and the hardest negatives dominate the\n398\t    gradient — this should maximally differentiate strategies. If strategies don''t\n399\t    differentiate at τ=0.05, they''re unlikely to at τ=0.1 or 0.2 (where the loss\n400\t    is smoother and hard negatives matter less). The results confirm this: τ=0.1 shows\n401\t    slightly larger positive deltas for Qwen (the warmer loss surface allows more\n402\t    exploration), but still non-significant. τ=0.2 makes the loss too smooth, suppressing\n403\t    all hard-negative signal. Importantly, strategy rankings are unstable across τ:\n404\t    embedding beats random at τ=0.1 but loses at τ=0.2, confirming that the null result\n405\t    is not an artifact of temperature choice — there is genuinely no robust strategy\n406\t    advantage.\n407\t\n408\t    '\n409\t  tables:\n410\t  - name: 'Table A1: Temperature × Strategy Interaction (R@1, Qwen)'\n411\t    content: 'τ    | random | embedding | dataflow | Δ emb-rand | Δ df-rand\n412\t\n413\t      -----|--------|-----------|----------|-----------|----------\n414\t\n415\t      0.05 | 0.5290 | 0.5310    | 0.5245   | +0.20     | −0.45\n416\t\n417\t      0.10 | 0.5215 | 0.5250    | 0.5200   | +0.35     | −0.15\n418\t\n419\t      0.20 | 0.5040 | 0.5030    | 0.5015   | −0.10     | −0.25\n420\t\n421\t      '\n422\t  figures_description:\n423\t  - 'Line plot: R@1 vs τ for each strategy. All three lines decline together as τ\n424\t    increases. Lines are nearly parallel with <0.5pp separation at any τ. No crossing\n425\t    pattern that would suggest a strategy-specific optimal temperature.'\n426\t  analysis: 'Temperature ablation confirms the null result is not regime-specific.\n427\t    The best overall R@1 is at τ=0.05 (sharpest), but strategy differences remain\n428\t    noise-level at all temperatures. τ=0.2 degrades all strategies uniformly by ~2.5pp,\n429\t    consistent with known contrastive learning theory (too-warm temperatures lose\n430\t    discriminative power). The instability of strategy rankings across τ is itself\n431\t    evidence against a true strategy effect — a real signal would produce consistent\n432\t    rankings regardless of temperature. Report in appendix as robustness check.\n433\t\n434\t    '\n435\t- name: Dynamic embedding mining baseline\n436\t  plan_id: plan_015\n437\t  description: 'Re-mine embedding negatives per epoch vs static (mine once from pretrained).\n438\t    1 seed × 2 encoders. Tests whether static mining confound explains null result.\n439\t\n440\t    '\n441\t  expected_results:\n442\t    qwen_dynamic_R1: 0.5315\n443\t    qwen_static_R1: 0.531\n444\t    qwen_dynamic_minus_static_pp: 0.05\n445\t    starcoder_dynamic_R1: 0.44\n446\t    starcoder_static_R1: 0.4395\n447\t    starcoder_dynamic_minus_static_pp: 0.05\n448\t    mining_time_overhead_pct: 340\n449\t  result_rationale: 'Dynamic mining re-computes negatives using the current encoder\n450\t    state each epoch, so negatives should be harder (adapted to the learned representation).\n451\t    However, in the worst case, this doesn''t help because: (1) the encoder barely\n452\t    moves during 3 epochs of LoRA fine-tuning — the pretrained representation is 99%\n453\t    preserved, so dynamic and static negatives are nearly identical (the cosine similarity\n454\t    between epoch-0 and epoch-3 embeddings for any given sample is >0.995); (2) any\n455\t    benefit from harder negatives is offset by training instability (the loss landscape\n456\t    shifts each epoch, preventing convergence to a stable optimum); (3) the 3.4× time\n457\t    overhead (re-encoding 44K instances per epoch) makes this impractical for no gain.\n458\t    The delta of +0.05pp for both encoders is effectively zero, confirming that the\n459\t    null result is not an artifact of static mining.\n460\t\n461\t    '\n462\t  tables:\n463\t  - name: 'Table A2: Static vs Dynamic Mining'\n464\t    content: 'Encoder   | Static R@1 | Dynamic R@1 | Δ (pp) | Time overhead\n465\t\n466\t      ----------|-----------|------------|--------|-------------\n467\t\n468\t      Qwen      | 0.5310    | 0.5315     | +0.05  | +340%\n469\t\n470\t      StarCoder | 0.4395    | 0.4400     | +0.05  | +340%\n471\t\n472\t      '\n473\t  figures_description:\n474\t  - 'Scatter plot: per-instance R@1 agreement between static and dynamic. 98.5% of\n475\t    instances have identical predictions. The 1.5% that differ split evenly (static\n476\t    wins some, dynamic wins others). No systematic pattern.'\n477\t  analysis: 'Dynamic mining is a non-factor. The encoder doesn''t change enough during\n478\t    LoRA fine-tuning to make dynamic negatives meaningfully different from static\n479\t    ones. This rules out the confound that our null result is caused by stale negatives.\n480\t    For the negative-result paper, this is useful: \"even the theoretically optimal\n481\t    mining schedule (dynamic) cannot rescue strategy differentiation.\"\n482\t\n483\t    '\n484\t- name: Bootstrap CI for SWE-bench Lite resolve rates\n485\t  plan_id: plan_016\n486\t  description: '10,000 bootstrap resamples for each strategy''s resolve rate on SWE-bench\n487\t    Lite (300 instances). Tests statistical significance of resolve rate deltas.\n488\t\n489\t    '\n490\t  expected_results:\n491\t    qwen_random_resolve_rate: 0.2467\n492\t    qwen_random_bootstrap_CI:\n493\t    - 0.198\n494\t    - 0.296\n495\t    qwen_embedding_resolve_rate: 0.25\n496\t    qwen_embedding_bootstrap_CI:\n497\t    - 0.201\n498\t    - 0.299\n499\t    qwen_dataflow_resolve_rate: 0.2433\n500\t    qwen_dataflow_bootstrap_CI:\n501\t    - 0.196\n502\t    - 0.292\n503\t    emb_minus_random_bootstrap_CI:\n504\t    - -0.048\n505\t    - 0.055\n506\t    df_minus_random_bootstrap_CI:\n507\t    - -0.055\n508\t    - 0.048\n509\t    any_comparison_significant: false\n510\t    bootstrap_CI_width_pp: 4.9\n511\t  result_rationale: 'With 300 binary outcomes and a base rate of ~25%, the binomial\n512\t    standard error is √(0.25×0.75/300) = 0.025, giving a 95% CI half-width of ~5pp.\n513\t    The strategy deltas (<0.5pp) are 10× smaller than the CI width. Bootstrap resampling\n514\t    confirms the analytical calculation: all pairwise CIs include zero, with p-values\n515\t    >0.5. This is the expected worst-case outcome: SWE-bench''s small N makes it impossible\n516\t    to detect sub-1pp retrieval differences in downstream task performance. Even a\n517\t    \"true\" 0.5pp retrieval improvement would need N≈60,000 instances to detect at\n518\t    α=0.05 in downstream resolve rate.\n519\t\n520\t    '\n521\t  tables:\n522\t  - name: 'Table 4 (extended): Bootstrap CIs for SWE-bench Resolve Rates'\n523\t    content: 'Comparison              | Δ (pp) | Bootstrap 95% CI | p-value\n524\t\n525\t      ------------------------|--------|------------------|--------\n526\t\n527\t      Qwen emb − random       | +0.33  | [−4.8, +5.5]    | 0.89\n528\t\n529\t      Qwen df − random        | −0.33  | [−5.5, +4.8]    | 0.89\n530\t\n531\t      SC emb − random         | −0.34  | [−5.5, +4.8]    | 0.89\n532\t\n533\t      SC df − random          | −0.67  | [−5.8, +4.5]    | 0.80\n534\t\n535\t      '\n536\t  figures_description:\n537\t  - Bootstrap distribution histograms for each pairwise delta. All approximately normal,\n538\t    centered near zero, with 95% bands spanning roughly ±5pp. The observed deltas\n539\t    are indistinguishable from the center of each distribution.\n540\t  analysis: 'Bootstrap CIs formalize what the point estimates already suggest: SWE-bench\n541\t    cannot discriminate between mining strategies at the effect sizes observed. This\n542\t    is not a power problem we can fix — it''s a fundamental limitation of using 300-instance\n543\t    downstream evaluation to detect <1pp retrieval differences. For the negative-result\n544\t    paper, this motivates the argument that practitioners should not over-invest in\n545\t    mining strategy optimization based on downstream benchmarks.\n546\t\n547\t    '\n548\t- name: BM25 negative baseline\n549\t  plan_id: plan_018\n550\t  description: 'Mine BM25-based hard negatives as 4th strategy. Train ≥1 seed × 2\n551\t    encoders. Compare R@1 with random/embedding/dataflow to test whether BM25 negatives\n552\t    match embedding negatives (which would prevent closing the CoCoHaNeRe argument).\n553\t\n554\t    '\n555\t  expected_results:\n556\t    qwen_bm25_R1: 0.5275\n557\t    qwen_bm25_95CI:\n558\t    - 0.515\n559\t    - 0.54\n560\t    starcoder_bm25_R1: 0.437\n561\t    starcoder_bm25_95CI:\n562\t    - 0.42\n563\t    - 0.454\n564\t    qwen_bm25_minus_random_pp: -0.15\n565\t    starcoder_bm25_minus_random_pp: -0.6\n566\t    qwen_bm25_minus_embedding_pp: -0.35\n567\t    starcoder_bm25_minus_embedding_pp: -0.25\n568\t    bm25_vs_embedding_p_value_qwen: 0.42\n569\t    bm25_vs_embedding_p_value_starcoder: 0.55\n570\t    bm25_minus_random_R1_pp: -0.38\n571\t    bm25_minus_random_CI:\n572\t    - -1.5\n573\t    - 0.7\n574\t  result_rationale: 'The worst case for the CoCoHaNeRe correction argument: BM25 negatives\n575\t    perform similarly to embedding negatives, making it impossible to claim that BM25-proxy\n576\t    was the source of CoCoHaNeRe''s reported harm. Why this happens: (1) at full scale\n577\t    with 44K training instances, BM25 top-k negatives and embedding-distance top-k\n578\t    negatives have ~60% overlap — high-BM25 documents tend to also have high embedding\n579\t    similarity because both capture lexical/semantic relatedness; (2) the non-overlapping\n580\t    40% doesn''t create enough differential training signal to separate the strategies;\n581\t    (3) CoCoHaNeRe''s reported -27pp harm was likely driven by other confounds (different\n582\t    training data scale, different encoder, different evaluation protocol) not captured\n583\t    by the BM25-vs-embedding distinction alone. In the worst case, BM25 negatives\n584\t    are slightly worse than random (−0.15pp Qwen, −0.60pp StarCoder) but the CIs include\n585\t    zero and overlap completely with embedding CIs. The CoCoHaNeRe correction argument\n586\t    cannot be closed: we can say \"BM25 and embedding negatives produce similar results\n587\t    in our setup\" but not \"BM25-proxy caused the harm.\"\n588\t\n589\t    '\n590\t  tables:\n591\t  - name: 'Table 3: 4-Strategy Comparison Including BM25 Baseline'\n592\t    content: 'Encoder   | random | embedding | dataflow | BM25   | BM25−emb (pp) |\n593\t      p\n594\t\n595\t      ----------|--------|-----------|----------|--------|---------------|------\n596\t\n597\t      Qwen      | 0.5290 | 0.5310    | 0.5245   | 0.5275 | −0.35         | 0.42\n598\t\n599\t      StarCoder | 0.4430 | 0.4395    | 0.4280   | 0.4370 | −0.25         | 0.55\n600\t\n601\t      '\n602\t  figures_description:\n603\t  - 'Bar chart: 4 strategies for each encoder. All bars within 1.5pp of each other.\n604\t    BM25 bars sit between random and embedding for both encoders. Error bars (bootstrap\n605\t    95% CI) overlap completely across all 4 strategies. Visual message: all strategies\n606\t    are equivalent.'\n607\t  - 'Venn diagram: overlap between BM25 top-3 negatives and embedding top-3 negatives\n608\t    per query. ~60% overlap. Caption explains this high overlap is why the strategies\n609\t    produce similar training outcomes.'\n610\t  analysis: 'The BM25 baseline delivers the worst possible outcome for the paper''s\n611\t    original framing. BM25 ≈ embedding negatives means we cannot attribute CoCoHaNeRe''s\n612\t    reported harm to their BM25-proxy methodology. The \"correction\" claim must be\n613\t    downgraded to an \"observation\": \"We note that CoCoHaNeRe''s reported large negative\n614\t    effect (−27pp) was not reproduced in our controlled setup with either BM25 or\n615\t    embedding negatives, suggesting the effect may be attributable to other experimental\n616\t    differences (scale, encoder, evaluation protocol) rather than the BM25-vs-embedding\n617\t    distinction specifically.\" This is much weaker than the original Claim 1 framing.\n618\t    For the negative-result paper, this finding actually strengthens the narrative:\n619\t    even the BM25-vs-embedding distinction that motivated the study turns out to be\n620\t    illusory at scale.\n621\t\n622\t    '\n623\t- name: Pool size sensitivity analysis\n624\t  plan_id: plan_019\n625\t  description: 'Evaluate strategy rankings at pool={50, 100, 200, 500} using M2 best\n626\t    checkpoints on SWE-smith validation.\n627\t\n628\t    '\n629\t  expected_results:\n630\t    pool_50:\n631\t      qwen_random_R1: 0.529\n632\t      qwen_embedding_R1: 0.531\n633\t      ranking:\n634\t      - embedding\n635\t      - random\n636\t      - dataflow\n637\t    pool_100:\n638\t      qwen_random_R1: 0.412\n639\t      qwen_embedding_R1: 0.4085\n640\t      ranking:\n641\t      - random\n642\t      - embedding\n643\t      - dataflow\n644\t    pool_200:\n645\t      qwen_random_R1: 0.324\n646\t      qwen_embedding_R1: 0.3265\n647\t      ranking:\n648\t      - embedding\n649\t      - dataflow\n650\t      - random\n651\t    pool_500:\n652\t      qwen_random_R1: 0.231\n653\t      qwen_embedding_R1: 0.2285\n654\t      ranking:\n655\t      - random\n656\t      - dataflow\n657\t      - embedding\n658\t    pool_ranking_stable_pct: 25\n659\t    ranking_flips: 3\n660\t    kendall_tau_50_vs_500: 0.33\n661\t  result_rationale: 'Pool size sensitivity exposes the fragility of strategy rankings.\n662\t    At pool=50 (current), embedding slightly leads — but this is the artificial setting.\n663\t    As pool grows: (1) the absolute R@1 drops substantially because finding the gold\n664\t    file among 200 or 500 candidates is genuinely harder; (2) strategy rankings become\n665\t    unstable because the relative ordering depends on which specific distractor files\n666\t    are added to the pool — at pool=500, many repos don''t have 500 files, so padding\n667\t    strategies introduce noise; (3) the models were trained with pool sizes effectively\n668\t    set by the negative sampling (3 negatives → effective pool ~4), so they''re not\n669\t    calibrated for large-pool ranking; (4) the ranking instability (Kendall τ = 0.33\n670\t    between pool=50 and pool=500) means the strategy comparison is not robust to evaluation\n671\t    protocol choices. Only 25% of the time does the pool=50 ranking agree with other\n672\t    pool sizes — well below the 50% threshold that would indicate stability.\n673\t\n674\t    '\n675\t  tables:\n676\t  - name: 'Table 7: Strategy Ranking Stability Across Pool Sizes (Qwen)'\n677\t    content: 'Pool | random R@1 | emb R@1  | df R@1   | Best strategy | Rank = pool50?\n678\t\n679\t      -----|-----------|----------|----------|---------------|---------------\n680\t\n681\t      50   | 0.5290    | 0.5310   | 0.5245   | embedding     | (reference)\n682\t\n683\t      100  | 0.4120    | 0.4085   | 0.4050   | random        | No\n684\t\n685\t      200  | 0.3240    | 0.3265   | 0.3250   | embedding     | Yes\n686\t\n687\t      500  | 0.2310    | 0.2285   | 0.2295   | random        | No\n688\t\n689\t      '\n690\t  - name: 'Table 7b: Strategy Ranking Stability (StarCoder)'\n691\t    content: 'Pool | random R@1 | emb R@1  | df R@1   | Best strategy | Rank = pool50?\n692\t\n693\t      -----|-----------|----------|----------|---------------|---------------\n694\t\n695\t      50   | 0.4430    | 0.4395   | 0.4280   | random        | (reference)\n696\t\n697\t      100  | 0.3410    | 0.3380   | 0.3310   | random        | Yes\n698\t\n699\t      200  | 0.2620    | 0.2640   | 0.2590   | embedding     | No\n700\t\n701\t      500  | 0.1780    | 0.1750   | 0.1770   | random        | Yes\n702\t\n703\t      '\n704\t  figures_description:\n705\t  - 'Line plot: R@1 vs pool size (log scale x-axis) for 3 strategies, both encoders.\n706\t    All lines decline monotonically. Lines cross multiple times, showing ranking instability.\n707\t    Shaded bands (bootstrap CI) overlap at every pool size.'\n708\t  - 'Heatmap: Kendall τ rank correlation between all pool size pairs. Most values\n709\t    between 0.0 and 0.67, with no pair reaching τ > 0.67. Average τ = 0.33.'\n710\t  analysis: 'Pool size sensitivity confirms the worst case: strategy rankings are\n711\t    unstable. Rankings flip 3 times across 4 pool sizes, with stability at only 25%.\n712\t    This means the paper''s strategy comparison is contingent on the evaluation protocol,\n713\t    not a property of the strategies themselves. For the negative-result paper, this\n714\t    is a strong finding: \"strategy rankings are not robust to pool size choice, suggesting\n715\t    that reported differences in prior work may also be evaluation artifacts.\" The\n716\t    practical takeaway: don''t optimize mining strategy based on a single pool size\n717\t    — the ranking you find may not hold at deployment scale.\n718\t\n719\t    '\n720\t- name: 'Ablation: 1-hop vs 2-hop vs 3-hop call-graph bounding'\n721\t  plan_id: plan_007\n722\t  description: 'Test whether expanding call-graph scope (1/2/3-hop) changes dataflow\n723\t    negative quality. 1 seed × Qwen.\n724\t\n725\t    '\n726\t  expected_results:\n727\t    hop1_R1: 0.523\n728\t    hop2_R1: 0.5245\n729\t    hop3_R1: 0.521\n730\t    hop2_minus_hop1_pp: 0.15\n731\t    hop3_minus_hop2_pp: -0.35\n732\t  result_rationale: 'In the worst case, hop count doesn''t matter because the dataflow\n733\t    strategy itself doesn''t differentiate from random. 1-hop produces the most conservative\n734\t    connected set (fewer files connected → more negatives available → negatives are\n735\t    closer to embedding-based selection). 2-hop (current) is slightly better because\n736\t    it captures inter-procedural dependencies. 3-hop over-expands the connected set,\n737\t    marking too many files as connected and leaving fewer high-quality negatives.\n738\t    But all three are within noise of each other and within noise of random (0.5290),\n739\t    confirming that the structural signal pathway is not the bottleneck — the bottleneck\n740\t    is that no mining strategy differentiates at scale.\n741\t\n742\t    '\n743\t  tables:\n744\t  - name: 'Table A3: Hop Count Ablation (Qwen, dataflow strategy)'\n745\t    content: 'Hops | Connected files/query | Negatives available | R@1    | Δ vs random\n746\t\n747\t      -----|----------------------|--------------------|---------|-----------\n748\t\n749\t      1    | 2.1                  | 47.9               | 0.5230 | −0.60pp\n750\t\n751\t      2    | 4.8                  | 45.2               | 0.5245 | −0.45pp\n752\t\n753\t      3    | 11.3                 | 38.7               | 0.5210 | −0.80pp\n754\t\n755\t      '\n756\t  figures_description:\n757\t  - 'Bar chart: R@1 for 3 hop counts + random baseline (dashed line). All bars below\n758\t    or at the dashed line. Minimal variation across hops.'\n759\t  analysis: 'Hop count ablation is uninformative in the worst case — it''s optimizing\n760\t    within a strategy that doesn''t work. The only useful observation: 3-hop over-expansion\n761\t    is slightly worse, suggesting that very broad connectivity definitions reduce\n762\t    negative quality. But the effect is tiny (0.35pp) and this entire analysis is\n763\t    moot given the overarching null result. Relegate to appendix.\n764\t\n765\t    '\n766\t- name: 'Ablation: training data size scaling'\n767\t  plan_id: plan_008\n768\t  description: '1K vs 3K vs 5K (full) training instances, all 3 strategies, 1 seed\n769\t    × Qwen. Tests whether strategy effects are scale-dependent.\n770\t\n771\t    '\n772\t  expected_results:\n773\t    size_1K:\n774\t      random_R1: 0.505\n775\t      embedding_R1: 0.518\n776\t      dataflow_R1: 0.512\n777\t      emb_minus_random_pp: 1.3\n778\t    size_3K:\n779\t      random_R1: 0.52\n780\t      embedding_R1: 0.526\n781\t      dataflow_R1: 0.521\n782\t      emb_minus_random_pp: 0.6\n783\t    size_5K:\n784\t      random_R1: 0.529\n785\t      embedding_R1: 0.531\n786\t      dataflow_R1: 0.5245\n787\t      emb_minus_random_pp: 0.2\n788\t    effect_trend: strategy advantage monotonically decreases with scale\n789\t  result_rationale: 'This is the most important ablation for the negative-result narrative.\n790\t    At 1K instances (pilot-like scale), embedding mining shows a +1.3pp advantage\n791\t    — close to the pilot''s +2.0pp, confirming that the pilot finding was real but\n792\t    scale-dependent. As training data increases to 3K and 5K, the advantage shrinks\n793\t    to 0.6pp and 0.2pp respectively. Mechanism: with more training data, the random\n794\t    negatives sample a wider swath of the embedding space, effectively approximating\n795\t    what curated embedding negatives provide. At small scale, curated negatives concentrate\n796\t    the training signal on the decision boundary; at large scale, random sampling\n797\t    covers the boundary adequately through sheer volume. This is a well-known phenomenon\n798\t    in contrastive learning (cf. SimCLR batch size scaling) and provides the mechanistic\n799\t    explanation for why pilot results didn''t replicate at full scale.\n800\t\n801\t    '\n802\t  tables:\n803\t  - name: 'Table 8: Scale-Dependent Strategy Effects'\n804\t    content: 'Train size | random R@1 | embedding R@1 | Δ (pp) | df R@1  | Δ df-rand\n805\t      (pp)\n806\t\n807\t      -----------|-----------|--------------|--------|---------|---------------\n808\t\n809\t      1K         | 0.5050    | 0.5180       | +1.30  | 0.5120  | +0.70\n810\t\n811\t      3K         | 0.5200    | 0.5260       | +0.60  | 0.5210  | +0.10\n812\t\n813\t      5K (full)  | 0.5290    | 0.5310       | +0.20  | 0.5245  | −0.45\n814\t\n815\t      '\n816\t  figures_description:\n817\t  - 'Line plot: Δ(embedding − random) vs training data size. Monotonically decreasing\n818\t    curve from +1.3pp at 1K to +0.2pp at 5K. Dashed horizontal line at 0. Extrapolation\n819\t    suggests the delta would cross zero around 6-7K instances. This is Figure 4 of\n820\t    the paper — the key evidence for the scale-dependent finding.'\n821\t  analysis: 'This is the paper''s most compelling experiment. The scale-dependent\n822\t    effect provides the mechanistic story: hard negative mining helps at small scale\n823\t    but its advantage vanishes as random sampling catches up. This connects to the\n824\t    representation geometry analysis: at small scale, random negatives are too easy\n825\t    (high margin) and the encoder doesn''t learn the decision boundary well. As scale\n826\t    increases, random negatives include enough hard examples by chance. This reframes\n827\t    the negative result as a positive finding: \"scale is a substitute for curation\n828\t    in contrastive code retrieval.\" Actionable takeaway: if your training set is small\n829\t    (<2K), invest in mining; if large (>5K), random suffices.\n830\t\n831\t    '\n832\t- name: 'Ablation: fusion head (embedding-only vs structural-only vs combined)'\n833\t  plan_id: plan_009\n834\t  description: 'Test whether adding structural features at inference time helps. 3\n835\t    heads × best checkpoint × Qwen.\n836\t\n837\t    '\n838\t  expected_results:\n839\t    embedding_only_R1: 0.531\n840\t    structural_only_R1: 0.312\n841\t    combined_R1: 0.5285\n842\t    combined_minus_embedding_pp: -0.25\n843\t  result_rationale: 'In the worst case, the structural signal is weak enough that\n844\t    adding it to the embedding at inference time actually hurts slightly (−0.25pp).\n845\t    The structural-only head (using only dataflow features) performs poorly (R@1=0.312)\n846\t    because structural connectivity alone is too coarse — many connected files are\n847\t    not relevant, and many relevant files are not connected. The combined head underperforms\n848\t    embedding-only because the structural features add noise: the model has to learn\n849\t    to ignore the structural channel for the 66% of instances without structural data,\n850\t    and this interferes with the embedding channel. This confirms that structural\n851\t    information is better used at training time (as negative selection criterion)\n852\t    than at inference time — but even at training time it doesn''t help in the worst\n853\t    case.\n854\t\n855\t    '\n856\t  tables:\n857\t  - name: 'Table A4: Fusion Head Ablation (Qwen)'\n858\t    content: 'Head           | R@1    | R@5    | R@10   | Δ vs emb-only\n859\t\n860\t      ---------------|--------|--------|--------|--------------\n861\t\n862\t      Embedding only | 0.5310 | 0.9600 | 1.000  | —\n863\t\n864\t      Structural only| 0.3120 | 0.6200 | 0.780  | −21.9pp\n865\t\n866\t      Combined       | 0.5285 | 0.9550 | 0.998  | −0.25pp\n867\t\n868\t      '\n869\t  figures_description:\n870\t  - Stacked bar chart showing contribution of each feature channel. Embedding channel\n871\t    dominates in all settings. Structural channel's learned weight approaches zero\n872\t    after training, confirming the model learns to ignore it.\n873\t  analysis: 'Fusion head ablation confirms structural features don''t add value at\n874\t    inference time. The model effectively learns to zero out the structural channel.\n875\t    This is consistent with the broader finding that structural information''s role\n876\t    in code retrieval is minimal when the training set is large enough.\n877\t\n878\t    '\n879\t- name: 'Ablation: dataflow-distance weighted negatives vs binary'\n880\t  plan_id: plan_010\n881\t  description: 'Compare binary connected/disconnected with distance-weighted negative\n882\t    selection (closer disconnected files weighted higher). 1 seed × Qwen.\n883\t\n884\t    '\n885\t  expected_results:\n886\t    binary_R1: 0.5245\n887\t    weighted_R1: 0.5255\n888\t    weighted_minus_binary_pp: 0.1\n889\t  result_rationale: 'Distance weighting provides slightly more informative negatives\n890\t    by preferring disconnected files that are structurally close (1-2 hops from connected\n891\t    boundary). But the improvement (+0.10pp) is negligible because: (1) the base dataflow\n892\t    strategy already doesn''t help, so refining it doesn''t matter; (2) the distance\n893\t    information from CodeQL is noisy (call-graph hop counts don''t perfectly correlate\n894\t    with structural relevance); (3) at 34% structural coverage, most instances don''t\n895\t    have distance information, so the weighting applies to a minority of training\n896\t    examples.\n897\t\n898\t    '\n899\t  tables:\n900\t  - name: 'Table A5: Distance Weighting Ablation'\n901\t    content: 'Negative selection | R@1    | Δ vs random | Δ vs binary\n902\t\n903\t      -------------------|--------|------------|-------------\n904\t\n905\t      Random             | 0.5290 | —          | —\n906\t\n907\t      Binary disconnect  | 0.5245 | −0.45pp   | —\n908\t\n909\t      Distance weighted  | 0.5255 | −0.35pp   | +0.10pp\n910\t\n911\t      '\n912\t  figures_description: []\n913\t  analysis: 'Marginal and uninformative. Confirms the dataflow pathway is not the\n914\t    lever. Appendix material only.\n915\t\n916\t    '\n917\t- name: 'Failure analysis: 50 retrieval failures'\n918\t  plan_id: plan_011\n919\t  description: 'Manual categorization of 50 cases where all strategies fail. Identify\n920\t    whether dataflow could have helped.\n921\t\n922\t    '\n923\t  expected_results:\n924\t    total_failures_analyzed: 50\n925\t    categories:\n926\t      gold_file_very_different: 18\n927\t      gold_file_in_different_language: 4\n928\t      config_or_test_files: 12\n929\t      very_large_repo_dilution: 8\n930\t      dataflow_could_help: 5\n931\t      ambiguous: 3\n932\t    dataflow_could_help_pct: 10\n933\t  result_rationale: 'Most retrieval failures are cases where the gold file is fundamentally\n934\t    different from the query (bug report → config file, error message → test file).\n935\t    These are not addressable by any negative mining strategy because the core problem\n936\t    is the query-document mismatch, not the contrastive training signal. Only ~10%\n937\t    of failures involve cases where structural connectivity could plausibly help (e.g.,\n938\t    gold file is structurally connected but lexically dissimilar). This low ceiling\n939\t    limits the potential upside of any structural approach.\n940\t\n941\t    '\n942\t  tables:\n943\t  - name: 'Table 9: Failure Category Breakdown'\n944\t    content: 'Category                        | Count | Pct  | Structural fix possible?\n945\t\n946\t      --------------------------------|-------|------|------------------------\n947\t\n948\t      Gold file lexically dissimilar  | 18    | 36%  | Unlikely\n949\t\n950\t      Config / test / non-code files  | 12    | 24%  | No\n951\t\n952\t      Large repo dilution (>500 files)| 8     | 16%  | Partial\n953\t\n954\t      Cross-language dependency       | 4     | 8%   | No\n955\t\n956\t      Structural connectivity helps   | 5     | 10%  | Yes\n957\t\n958\t      Ambiguous                       | 3     | 6%   | Maybe\n959\t\n960\t      '\n961\t  figures_description:\n962\t  - Pie chart of failure categories. Largest slice (36%) is 'lexically dissimilar\n963\t    gold file.' Only 10% slice is 'structural fix possible.'\n964\t  analysis: 'Failure analysis provides the ceiling argument for the negative-result\n965\t    paper: even a perfect structural negative mining strategy could only address ~10%\n966\t    of failure cases. The dominant failure mode (lexically dissimilar gold files)\n967\t    requires fundamentally different approaches (e.g., multi-modal queries that include\n968\t    error traces, stack traces, or issue descriptions). This explains *why* structural\n969\t    negatives don''t help at scale: the failure distribution is dominated by cases\n970\t    where structure is irrelevant.\n971\t\n972\t    '\n973\t- name: 'Qualitative examples: strategy comparison'\n974\t  plan_id: plan_012\n975\t  description: '5-10 cases where strategies produce different retrieval outcomes.\n976\t    Cherry-pick both directions (structural helps, structural hurts).\n977\t\n978\t    '\n979\t  expected_results:\n980\t    examples_found: 7\n981\t    structural_helps: 2\n982\t    structural_hurts: 3\n983\t    no_clear_pattern: 2\n984\t  result_rationale: 'In the worst case, qualitative examples cannot construct a compelling\n985\t    narrative because the effects go both directions. The 2 cases where structural\n986\t    negatives help are genuine (query about a function call, gold file is the callee\n987\t    2 hops away), but the 3 cases where they hurt are equally compelling (structural\n988\t    negatives push the encoder to distinguish files that are actually both relevant,\n989\t    causing it to miss the gold file). The balanced count prevents cherry-picking\n990\t    a one-directional story.\n991\t\n992\t    '\n993\t  tables: []\n994\t  figures_description:\n995\t  - Side-by-side retrieval rank comparison for 4 example queries. Two examples show\n996\t    dataflow improving rank (rank 8→3, rank 12→5). Two show dataflow worsening rank\n997\t    (rank 2→7, rank 4→11). Presented honestly with both directions.\n998\t  analysis: 'Qualitative examples support the negative-result narrative: strategy\n999\t    choice cuts both ways, with no systematic direction. Use 2-3 examples in the paper\n1000\t    to illustrate *why* strategies don''t differentiate, not to argue for one strategy.\n1001\t\n1002\t    '\n1003\t- name: Inference-time graph method comparison (analysis only)\n1004\t  plan_id: plan_017\n1005\t  description: 'Literature analysis comparing training-time (StructFuse) vs inference-time\n1006\t    (CodexGraph, LocAgent, RANGER) graph-based approaches. No experiment.\n1007\t\n1008\t    '\n1009\t  expected_results:\n1010\t    papers_surveyed: 8\n1011\t    conclusion: complementary in theory, but our null result suggests training-time\n1012\t      structural signal may be less valuable than inference-time navigation\n1013\t    key_finding: 'Inference-time methods re-compute structural relevance per query\n1014\t      using the actual query context, while training-time methods (ours) bake structural\n1015\t      signal into the encoder via negative selection. Our null result suggests the\n1016\t      baked-in signal gets washed out, while inference-time methods preserve it. This\n1017\t      reframes our contribution: training-time structure is less effective than inference-time\n1018\t      structure for code retrieval.\n1019\t\n1020\t      '\n1021\t  result_rationale: 'The literature comparison is more damaging in the worst case\n1022\t    than it appears. If our structural mining doesn''t help, but inference-time graph\n1023\t    methods (LocAgent +8-12pp on SWE-bench, CodexGraph +5pp) do help, the implication\n1024\t    is that structure matters but our approach to incorporating it (training-time\n1025\t    negative selection) is the wrong mechanism. This shifts the paper from \"structure\n1026\t    helps conditionally\" to \"training-time structure doesn''t help, and we explain\n1027\t    why inference-time approaches succeed where we fail.\" The explanation: training-time\n1028\t    mining fixes the structural signal at corpus construction time, but the relevant\n1029\t    structure changes per query (different bugs involve different call chains). Inference-time\n1030\t    methods adapt to the query.\n1031\t\n1032\t    '\n1033\t  tables: []\n1034\t  figures_description:\n1035\t  - 'Comparison table: training-time vs inference-time structural approaches. Columns:\n1036\t    method, when structure is used, adaptation to query, reported gains. Shows inference-time\n1037\t    methods consistently report larger gains.'\n1038\t  analysis: 'This comparison is uncomfortable but essential for the negative-result\n1039\t    paper. It provides the theoretical explanation for our null result: static structural\n1040\t    mining at training time cannot capture query-specific structural relevance. The\n1041\t    practical takeaway: if you want to leverage code structure for retrieval, invest\n1042\t    in inference-time graph navigation (CodexGraph, LocAgent), not training-time negative\n1043\t    mining. This is an actionable, non-obvious conclusion that justifies the paper''s\n1044\t    contribution.\n1045\t\n1046\t    '\n1047\t- name: Representation geometry convergence analysis\n1048\t  plan_id: 'plan_005 (extended: post-training geometry)'\n1049\t  description: 'Measure pos_sim, neg_sim, margin for all strategies using fine-tuned\n1050\t    encoders (not just pretrained). Tests whether fine-tuning erases pretrained geometry\n1051\t    differences.\n1052\t\n1053\t    '\n1054\t  expected_results:\n1055\t    qwen_post_finetune:\n1056\t      random:\n1057\t        pos_sim: 0.912\n1058\t        neg_sim: 0.845\n1059\t        margin: 0.067\n1060\t      embedding:\n1061\t        pos_sim: 0.9135\n1062\t        neg_sim: 0.862\n1063\t        margin: 0.0515\n1064\t      dataflow:\n1065\t        pos_sim: 0.9128\n1066\t        neg_sim: 0.854\n1067\t        margin: 0.0588\n1068\t    starcoder_post_finetune:\n1069\t      random:\n1070\t        pos_sim: 0.865\n1071\t        neg_sim: 0.792\n1072\t        margin: 0.073\n1073\t      embedding:\n1074\t        pos_sim: 0.867\n1075\t        neg_sim: 0.81\n1076\t        margin: 0.057\n1077\t      dataflow:\n1078\t        pos_sim: 0.866\n1079\t        neg_sim: 0.801\n1080\t        margin: 0.065\n1081\t    margin_convergence_qwen_pct: 78\n1082\t    margin_convergence_starcoder_pct: 65\n1083\t  result_rationale: 'Fine-tuning collapses the pretrained geometry differences. Pre-fine-tuning,\n1084\t    Qwen (pos_sim=0.986) and StarCoder (pos_sim=0.798) had dramatically different\n1085\t    geometries. Post-fine-tuning, both converge toward pos_sim ~0.87-0.91. The margins\n1086\t    across strategies also converge: pretrained Qwen had random margin 0.074 vs embedding\n1087\t    margin 0.017 (4.4× ratio); post-fine-tuning, random margin 0.067 vs embedding\n1088\t    margin 0.052 (1.3× ratio). Fine-tuning acts as a geometry equalizer, pushing all\n1089\t    representations toward the contrastive loss optimum regardless of the pretrained\n1090\t    starting point. This explains why strategy differences that existed in the pretrained\n1091\t    space don''t translate to performance differences after training: the encoder\n1092\t    adjusts its geometry to optimize the contrastive objective, and the specific negatives\n1093\t    used matter less than the objective function itself. For Qwen, 78% of the pretrained\n1094\t    margin difference between strategies is erased by fine-tuning. For StarCoder,\n1095\t    65% is erased.\n1096\t\n1097\t    '\n1098\t  tables:\n1099\t  - name: 'Table 10: Representation Geometry Pre vs Post Fine-tuning'\n1100\t    content: 'Encoder   | Phase      | Metric     | random | embedding | dataflow\n1101\t      | Ratio(rand/emb)\n1102\t\n1103\t      ----------|------------|------------|--------|-----------|----------|---------------\n1104\t\n1105\t      Qwen      | Pretrained | margin     | 0.074  | 0.017     | 0.029    | 4.35\n1106\t\n1107\t      Qwen      | Fine-tuned | margin     | 0.067  | 0.052     | 0.059    | 1.29\n1108\t\n1109\t      StarCoder | Pretrained | margin     | 0.194  | 0.067     | 0.119    | 2.90\n1110\t\n1111\t      StarCoder | Fine-tuned | margin     | 0.073  | 0.057     | 0.065    | 1.28\n1112\t\n1113\t      '\n1114\t  figures_description:\n1115\t  - 'Figure 5 (key paper figure): Margin distribution histograms pre vs post fine-tuning,\n1116\t    3 strategies overlaid. Pre-fine-tuning: clearly separated distributions. Post-fine-tuning:\n1117\t    distributions converge to similar shape and location. Title: ''Fine-tuning erases\n1118\t    pretrained geometry differences.'' This is the mechanistic explanation figure.'\n1119\t  - 'Scatter plot: pretrained margin vs post-training margin for each instance, colored\n1120\t    by strategy. Points cluster around a diagonal but with compression toward a common\n1121\t    band, showing convergence.'\n1122\t  analysis: 'This is the paper''s strongest contribution in the worst case: the mechanistic\n1123\t    explanation for why strategy choice doesn''t matter. Fine-tuning acts as a geometry\n1124\t    equalizer — regardless of which negatives you use, the contrastive objective pushes\n1125\t    the encoder toward the same representation geometry. The pretrained geometry (which\n1126\t    determines initial negative difficulty) is a transient condition that gets erased.\n1127\t    This explains why: (1) pilot-scale results showed strategy effects (not enough\n1128\t    training to fully erase geometry), (2) full-scale results don''t (enough training\n1129\t    to converge), (3) temperature doesn''t matter (the geometry convergence is objective-driven,\n1130\t    not temperature-dependent). The representation geometry analysis transforms the\n1131\t    negative result from \"nothing works\" to \"everything converges\" — a much more publishable\n1132\t    framing.'\n1133\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66320d78-4e7d-4d1c-acee-fe19e7e4f482","uuid":"0f849922-c0b1-4a64-a48e-0e79f556702b","timestamp":"2026-05-14T17:14:37.668Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/structfuse-context/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T162530/worst/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'Full-scale effect sizes collapse. Embedding-distance mining shows\n  no reliable advantage over random baselines once multi-seed CIs are computed; dataflow\n  negatives show no benefit or slight harm on both encoders. BM25 negatives perform\n  statistically indistinguishable from embedding negatives, preventing closure of\n  the CoCoHaNeRe correction argument. Pool size scaling destabilizes strategy rankings.\n  SWE-bench shows no downstream signal. The paper must pivot to a negative-result\n  framing: \"Why Hard Negative Mining Strategies Don''t Differentiate for Code Retrieval\n  at Scale.\" The salvageable contribution is the representation geometry analysis\n  explaining *why* strategies converge (margins collapse post-fine-tuning), plus the\n  methodological caution about BM25 proxies (downgraded from \"correction\" to \"observation\").\n\n  '\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: M2 Full Qwen 9-seed 3-strategy training\n  plan_id: plan_002 / m2_full_qwen\n  description: '9 seeds × 3 strategies (random, embedding_distance, dataflow_aware)\n    on Qwen2.5-Coder-3B LoRA. Full training data (~44K instances). Pool=50 eval.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    random_mean_R1: 0.529\n    random_std_R1: 0.0085\n    random_95CI:\n    - 0.5224\n    - 0.5356\n    embedding_distance_mean_R1: 0.531\n    embedding_distance_std_R1: 0.0092\n    embedding_distance_95CI:\n    - 0.5239\n    - 0.5381\n    dataflow_aware_mean_R1: 0.5245\n    dataflow_aware_std_R1: 0.011\n    dataflow_aware_95CI:\n    - 0.516\n    - 0.533\n    emb_minus_random_R1_pp: 0.2\n    emb_minus_random_CI:\n    - -0.62\n    - 1.02\n    df_minus_random_R1_pp: -0.45\n    df_minus_random_CI:\n    - -1.5\n    - 0.6\n    best_step_mode: 1000\n    best_step_range:\n    - 500\n    - 1500\n    overfit_onset_step: 1500\n  result_rationale: 'Pilot showed emb +2.0pp over random (0.5557 vs 0.5357), but that\n    was 1 seed on pilot-scale data. At full scale with 9 seeds, seed variance dominates\n    the small effect. The 2.0pp pilot delta shrinks to ~0.2pp because: (1) full training\n    data (44K vs pilot subset) gives random negatives more coverage of the embedding\n    space, reducing the advantage of curated hard negatives; (2) Qwen''s extremely\n    saturated pretrained space (pos_sim=0.9855) means all strategies produce negatives\n    that are very close to the query — the margin between random_neg_sim (0.911) and\n    emb_neg_sim (0.969) gets compressed further during fine-tuning as the encoder\n    adjusts; (3) with 9 seeds, some seeds favor random (data order effects), some\n    favor embedding, and the mean converges. The CI clearly includes 0, killing the\n    claim that embedding mining \"consistently improves\" retrieval. Dataflow negatives\n    show slight harm because at full scale, the 34% structural coverage means 66%\n    of training instances fall back to random, diluting any signal; the structural\n    negatives for the remaining 34% may be less informative than embedding negatives\n    (dataflow disconnection doesn''t guarantee semantic difficulty).\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 2: Full-Scale R@1 by Strategy × Encoder (Qwen)'\n    content: 'Strategy          | Mean R@1 | Std   | 95% CI          | Δ vs Random\n      | p (paired-t)\n\n      ------------------|----------|-------|-----------------|-------------|-------------\n\n      random            | 0.5290   | 0.009 | [0.522, 0.536]  | —           | —\n\n      embedding_dist    | 0.5310   | 0.009 | [0.524, 0.538]  | +0.20pp     | 0.58\n\n      dataflow_aware    | 0.5245   | 0.011 | [0.516, 0.533]  | −0.45pp     | 0.31\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - Box plot of R@1 across 9 seeds for 3 strategies (Qwen). Boxes heavily overlap.\n    Median lines for random and embedding nearly coincident. Dataflow median slightly\n    below but within IQR of both others.\n  - Learning curves (val_loss vs step) for all 27 runs, color-coded by strategy. All\n    strategies converge to similar loss band [0.46–0.54] by step 1000, with divergence\n    only in overfitting tail. No strategy consistently lower.\n  analysis: 'The pilot''s +2.0pp embedding advantage was a seed-specific fluctuation.\n    At full scale, the effect is +0.20pp with p=0.58 — no statistical significance.\n    Dataflow shows a slight negative trend (−0.45pp) but also non-significant. Seed-to-seed\n    variance (σ ≈ 0.009) exceeds the strategy effect, making it impossible to distinguish\n    strategies on this encoder. This is consistent with Qwen''s extreme representation\n    saturation: when pos_sim=0.986, fine-tuning rapidly compresses any initial margin\n    differences, and all strategies end up in the same loss basin.\n\n    '\n- name: M2 Full StarCoder 9-seed 3-strategy training\n  plan_id: plan_002 / m2_full_starcoder\n  description: '9 seeds × 3 strategies on StarCoder2-3B. Same protocol as Qwen.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    random_mean_R1: 0.443\n    random_std_R1: 0.0105\n    random_95CI:\n    - 0.4349\n    - 0.4511\n    embedding_distance_mean_R1: 0.4395\n    embedding_distance_std_R1: 0.012\n    embedding_distance_95CI:\n    - 0.4302\n    - 0.4488\n    dataflow_aware_mean_R1: 0.428\n    dataflow_aware_std_R1: 0.0135\n    dataflow_aware_95CI:\n    - 0.4176\n    - 0.4384\n    emb_minus_random_R1_pp: -0.35\n    emb_minus_random_CI:\n    - -1.4\n    - 0.7\n    df_minus_random_R1_pp: -1.5\n    df_minus_random_CI:\n    - -3.1\n    - 0.1\n    best_step_mode: 1000\n    overfit_onset_step: 1500\n  result_rationale: 'StarCoder pilot showed embedding +1.56pp (0.5078 vs 0.4922),\n    but pilot used post-hoc eval for random (no in-training eval), introducing measurement\n    inconsistency. At full scale with 9 seeds and consistent eval: (1) StarCoder''s\n    healthier pretrained geometry (pos_sim=0.80) means random negatives already provide\n    decent margin (0.194), so embedding mining''s tighter margins (0.067) may actually\n    hurt by making the contrastive task too hard early in training, causing some seeds\n    to find worse optima; (2) the higher seed variance for StarCoder (σ=0.012 vs Qwen''s\n    0.009) reflects the decoder-only architecture''s sensitivity to initialization\n    — FIM pretraining creates a less smooth loss landscape for retrieval fine-tuning;\n    (3) dataflow negatives show the most harm (−1.50pp) because StarCoder''s intact\n    geometry already separates structural from non-structural code well, so forcing\n    structural discrimination adds noise rather than signal. The CI for embedding\n    nearly spans zero but slightly favors negative; for dataflow, the CI upper bound\n    just barely touches zero, suggesting possible marginal harm.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 2 (continued): Full-Scale R@1 by Strategy × Encoder (StarCoder)'\n    content: 'Strategy          | Mean R@1 | Std   | 95% CI          | Δ vs Random\n      | p (paired-t)\n\n      ------------------|----------|-------|-----------------|-------------|-------------\n\n      random            | 0.4430   | 0.011 | [0.435, 0.451]  | —           | —\n\n      embedding_dist    | 0.4395   | 0.012 | [0.430, 0.449]  | −0.35pp     | 0.47\n\n      dataflow_aware    | 0.4280   | 0.014 | [0.418, 0.438]  | −1.50pp     | 0.015\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - Box plot (StarCoder). Random and embedding boxes overlap almost entirely. Dataflow\n    box shifted lower, with 2/9 seeds performing significantly worse (outliers below\n    Q1). Suggests dataflow introduces training instability for some seeds.\n  analysis: 'StarCoder''s full-scale results are worse than Qwen overall (R@1 ~0.44\n    vs ~0.53), consistent with decoder-only FIM pretraining being less suited for\n    bidirectional retrieval. Critically, embedding mining does not help — the sign\n    flipped from pilot (+1.56pp → −0.35pp). Dataflow shows possible harm (p=0.015),\n    but with 9 comparisons across the study, this doesn''t survive multiple-testing\n    correction (Bonferroni threshold ~0.006). The dominant finding is that no strategy\n    differentiates reliably. Combined with Qwen, neither encoder shows a strategy\n    effect that survives multi-seed evaluation.\n\n    '\n- name: M3a SWE-bench Lite end-to-end evaluation\n  plan_id: plan_003\n  description: '3 strategies × best checkpoint per encoder, evaluated on SWE-bench\n    Lite (300 instances) with Qwen3.6-27B agent. Measures resolve rate.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    qwen_random_resolve_rate: 0.2467\n    qwen_embedding_resolve_rate: 0.25\n    qwen_dataflow_resolve_rate: 0.2433\n    starcoder_random_resolve_rate: 0.2267\n    starcoder_embedding_resolve_rate: 0.2233\n    starcoder_dataflow_resolve_rate: 0.22\n    best_minus_worst_pp: 0.67\n    swebench_resolve_rate_delta_pp: 0.33\n    qwen_emb_minus_random_delta_pp: 0.33\n    starcoder_emb_minus_random_delta_pp: -0.34\n  result_rationale: 'SWE-bench resolve rate is dominated by the LLM agent''s reasoning\n    ability, not retrieval quality. The retrieval step provides a candidate set, but\n    the agent re-ranks and explores beyond the initial retrieval. With R@1 differences\n    of <0.5pp at pool=50, the downstream task absorbs the noise entirely. Quantitatively:\n    (1) at resolve rates of ~24%, a 1pp R@1 improvement translates to at most ~0.3pp\n    resolve rate improvement (retrieval → localization → patch → test is a multi-stage\n    pipeline where errors compound but also get corrected by the agent); (2) with\n    300 instances, the binomial CI for a 24% resolve rate is ±4.8pp — any sub-1pp\n    strategy difference is invisible; (3) the agent''s exploration strategy (re-reading\n    files, searching for imports) effectively compensates for missed retrievals, especially\n    for the dataflow-relevant cases where structural dependencies guide the agent''s\n    natural exploration. The numbers bounce randomly around ~24% with no interpretable\n    pattern.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 4: SWE-bench Lite Resolve Rate'\n    content: 'Encoder   | Strategy   | Resolve Rate | Δ vs Random | 95% Binom CI\n\n      ----------|------------|-------------|-------------|------------------\n\n      Qwen      | random     | 24.67%      | —           | [19.9%, 29.4%]\n\n      Qwen      | embedding  | 25.00%      | +0.33pp     | [20.2%, 29.8%]\n\n      Qwen      | dataflow   | 24.33%      | −0.33pp     | [19.6%, 29.1%]\n\n      StarCoder | random     | 22.67%      | —           | [18.0%, 27.3%]\n\n      StarCoder | embedding  | 22.33%      | −0.34pp     | [17.7%, 27.0%]\n\n      StarCoder | dataflow   | 22.00%      | −0.67pp     | [17.3%, 26.7%]\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - Bar chart with error bars. All 6 bars are between 22% and 25%, with CI whiskers\n    spanning 18–30%. No visible separation. A dashed line at the SweRank reported\n    rate (26%) sits within all CIs.\n  analysis: 'No downstream differentiation. The largest gap (Qwen-embedding vs StarCoder-dataflow)\n    is 2.8pp, which is well within the ±5pp binomial CI. This confirms the worst-case\n    prediction: when retrieval strategy differences are <0.5pp, the downstream task\n    cannot detect them. The paper cannot claim any practical benefit from mining strategy\n    choice. SWE-bench results must be reported transparently as showing no differentiation,\n    which strengthens the negative-result narrative (\"practitioners need not invest\n    GPU time in fancy mining\").\n\n    '\n- name: M3b RepoBench-R cross-file retrieval evaluation\n  plan_id: plan_004\n  description: 'Retrieval-only evaluation on RepoBench-R (test_easy ~8K, test_hard\n    ~8K). 3 strategies × 2 encoders × 9 seeds. Report R@1 with 95% bootstrap CIs.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    qwen_easy_random_mean_R1: 0.712\n    qwen_easy_embedding_mean_R1: 0.715\n    qwen_easy_dataflow_mean_R1: 0.709\n    qwen_hard_random_mean_R1: 0.431\n    qwen_hard_embedding_mean_R1: 0.434\n    qwen_hard_dataflow_mean_R1: 0.427\n    starcoder_easy_random_mean_R1: 0.685\n    starcoder_easy_embedding_mean_R1: 0.681\n    starcoder_easy_dataflow_mean_R1: 0.678\n    starcoder_hard_random_mean_R1: 0.398\n    starcoder_hard_embedding_mean_R1: 0.393\n    starcoder_hard_dataflow_mean_R1: 0.389\n    repobench_R1_delta_pp: 0.3\n    bootstrap_CI_width_R1_pp: 3.5\n  result_rationale: 'RepoBench-R uses naturally small pools (~6 for easy, ~18 for\n    hard), which compresses retrieval difficulty. At pool=6, even a mediocre encoder\n    achieves high R@1 (>0.70 on easy), leaving little room for strategy differentiation.\n    The training data (SWE-smith) and eval data (RepoBench-R) come from different\n    distributions — SWE-smith is bug-fix focused while RepoBench is cross-file completion\n    — so training signal transfer is lossy. With 9 seeds and bootstrap CIs, the inter-seed\n    variance (~2-3pp) dwarfs the strategy effect (<0.5pp). The hard split shows more\n    variance but still no consistent direction: embedding helps Qwen by 0.3pp but\n    hurts StarCoder by 0.5pp, consistent with the full-scale result that strategy\n    effects are noise. Dataflow shows slight consistent harm because the structural\n    signal from SWE-smith repos doesn''t transfer to RepoBench''s different repo distribution.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 5: RepoBench-R R@1 by Strategy × Encoder × Split'\n    content: 'Split | Encoder   | random       | embedding    | dataflow     | Δ emb-rand\n\n      ------|-----------|-------------|-------------|-------------|------------\n\n      easy  | Qwen      | 71.2 ± 1.4  | 71.5 ± 1.5  | 70.9 ± 1.6  | +0.3pp\n\n      easy  | StarCoder | 68.5 ± 1.6  | 68.1 ± 1.7  | 67.8 ± 1.8  | −0.4pp\n\n      hard  | Qwen      | 43.1 ± 2.0  | 43.4 ± 2.1  | 42.7 ± 2.2  | +0.3pp\n\n      hard  | StarCoder | 39.8 ± 2.2  | 39.3 ± 2.4  | 38.9 ± 2.5  | −0.5pp\n\n      Note: ± indicates 95% bootstrap CI half-width. All CIs overlap across strategies.\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - Forest plot showing strategy deltas (embedding−random, dataflow−random) for both\n    encoders × both splits. All 8 CIs cross zero. No consistent direction across encoders.\n  analysis: 'RepoBench-R confirms the SWE-smith full-scale finding: no strategy reliably\n    differentiates on an external benchmark. The inconsistent sign across encoders\n    (positive for Qwen, negative for StarCoder) is the signature of noise, not signal.\n    CI widths of 3–5pp completely swamp the 0.3–0.5pp strategy effects. This external\n    validation strengthens the negative-result claim: strategy choice doesn''t matter\n    even when measured on a different benchmark with different pool sizes and task\n    formulation.\n\n    '\n- name: 'Stratified analysis: 5-stratum deep dive'\n  plan_id: plan_005\n  description: '5 strata: (1) repo size, (2) pool size, (3) query-positive similarity,\n    (4) structural data availability (34% vs 66%), (5) neg_difficulty quartile. Report\n    per-stratum R@1 deltas for all 3 strategies × 2 encoders.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    struct_available_emb_minus_random_qwen: 0.15\n    struct_available_df_minus_random_qwen: 0.4\n    struct_fallback_emb_minus_random_qwen: 0.25\n    struct_fallback_df_minus_random_qwen: -0.9\n    neg_diff_Q1_easy_emb_delta: 0.6\n    neg_diff_Q4_hard_emb_delta: -0.3\n    repo_small_emb_delta: 0.8\n    repo_large_emb_delta: -0.15\n    pool_small_emb_delta: 0.35\n    pool_large_emb_delta: -0.2\n    no_stratum_significant_after_correction: true\n  result_rationale: 'Stratified analysis reveals that the aggregate null result masks\n    opposing sub-population effects that cancel out — but none are individually significant\n    after multiple-testing correction. Structural data stratum: the 34% with dataflow\n    data shows a tiny positive df effect (+0.4pp), but the 66% fallback (which uses\n    random negatives labeled as \"dataflow\") dilutes and reverses it. This is expected\n    because the fallback mechanism means the \"dataflow\" strategy is actually ~66%\n    random, making the aggregate comparison (dataflow vs random) a comparison of (0.34×structural\n    + 0.66×random) vs (1.0×random). The 34% pure structural signal is too diluted\n    to surface. Neg_difficulty quartiles: the easiest quartile (Q1, high margin) shows\n    slight embedding advantage because the encoder can exploit the difficulty gradient;\n    the hardest quartile (Q4, near-zero margin) shows slight embedding harm because\n    those negatives are false-negative-adjacent (too similar to be true negatives).\n    But with 9 seeds, the per-quartile CIs are wide (~5pp) because each quartile has\n    only ~25% of the data. Repo size and pool size strata show similar noisy, contradictory\n    patterns.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 6: Per-Stratum Δ(embedding − random) R@1 (Qwen, pp)'\n    content: 'Stratum                  | N instances | Δ R@1 (pp) | 95% CI         |\n      Sig?\n\n      -------------------------|-------------|-----------|----------------|-----\n\n      Repo small (<200)        |  4596       | +0.80     | [−1.5, +3.1]   | No\n\n      Repo large (≥200)        | 39577       | −0.15     | [−0.9, +0.6]   | No\n\n      Pool small (<30)         | 12044       | +0.35     | [−1.2, +1.9]   | No\n\n      Pool large (≥30)         | 32129       | −0.20     | [−1.0, +0.6]   | No\n\n      High q-pos sim (>0.95)   | 28800       | −0.10     | [−0.8, +0.6]   | No\n\n      Low q-pos sim (≤0.95)    | 15373       | +0.55     | [−0.9, +2.0]   | No\n\n      Struct available (34%)   | 15133       | +0.15     | [−1.2, +1.5]   | No\n\n      Struct fallback (66%)    | 29040       | +0.25     | [−0.5, +1.0]   | No\n\n      Neg diff Q1 (easiest)    | 11043       | +0.60     | [−1.5, +2.7]   | No\n\n      Neg diff Q4 (hardest)    | 11043       | −0.30     | [−2.3, +1.7]   | No\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Heatmap: 5 strata × 2 levels × 3 strategy comparisons. Color scale from blue\n    (−2pp) to red (+2pp). Mostly pale/neutral colors. No consistent hot spot. Visual\n    message: no sub-population rescues the finding.'\n  - Per-stratum forest plot for dataflow−random on structural-available vs fallback\n    sub-populations. The structural-available CI is [−0.8, +1.6], overlapping zero.\n    The fallback CI is [−1.8, +0.0], suggesting possible harm from the fallback mechanism\n    but not significant.\n  analysis: 'No stratum produces a significant strategy effect after Bonferroni correction\n    (10 comparisons, α=0.005). The structural availability stratum is the most informative:\n    it shows that the 34% with real structural data has a tiny positive dataflow signal,\n    but this is completely washed out by the 66% fallback. This is actually a useful\n    finding for the negative-result paper: it suggests that structural negative mining\n    *might* help if structural data coverage were higher (>80%), but at practical\n    extraction rates (34%), the benefit is undetectable. This becomes an actionable\n    takeaway: \"invest in extraction coverage before investing in mining strategy.\"\n\n    '\n- name: 'Temperature ablation: τ ∈ {0.05, 0.1, 0.2}'\n  plan_id: plan_014\n  description: '3 temperatures × 3 strategies × 2 encoders. Tests whether the null\n    result is regime-specific to τ=0.05.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    tau_005_emb_minus_random_qwen: 0.2\n    tau_010_emb_minus_random_qwen: 0.35\n    tau_020_emb_minus_random_qwen: -0.1\n    tau_005_emb_minus_random_starcoder: -0.35\n    tau_010_emb_minus_random_starcoder: 0.15\n    tau_020_emb_minus_random_starcoder: -0.55\n    best_tau_overall: 0.05\n    strategy_ranking_changes_across_tau: true\n    any_tau_significant: false\n  result_rationale: 'Temperature controls the sharpness of the contrastive loss. At\n    τ=0.05 (current), the loss is very sharp and the hardest negatives dominate the\n    gradient — this should maximally differentiate strategies. If strategies don''t\n    differentiate at τ=0.05, they''re unlikely to at τ=0.1 or 0.2 (where the loss\n    is smoother and hard negatives matter less). The results confirm this: τ=0.1 shows\n    slightly larger positive deltas for Qwen (the warmer loss surface allows more\n    exploration), but still non-significant. τ=0.2 makes the loss too smooth, suppressing\n    all hard-negative signal. Importantly, strategy rankings are unstable across τ:\n    embedding beats random at τ=0.1 but loses at τ=0.2, confirming that the null result\n    is not an artifact of temperature choice — there is genuinely no robust strategy\n    advantage.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table A1: Temperature × Strategy Interaction (R@1, Qwen)'\n    content: 'τ    | random | embedding | dataflow | Δ emb-rand | Δ df-rand\n\n      -----|--------|-----------|----------|-----------|----------\n\n      0.05 | 0.5290 | 0.5310    | 0.5245   | +0.20     | −0.45\n\n      0.10 | 0.5215 | 0.5250    | 0.5200   | +0.35     | −0.15\n\n      0.20 | 0.5040 | 0.5030    | 0.5015   | −0.10     | −0.25\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Line plot: R@1 vs τ for each strategy. All three lines decline together as τ\n    increases. Lines are nearly parallel with <0.5pp separation at any τ. No crossing\n    pattern that would suggest a strategy-specific optimal temperature.'\n  analysis: 'Temperature ablation confirms the null result is not regime-specific.\n    The best overall R@1 is at τ=0.05 (sharpest), but strategy differences remain\n    noise-level at all temperatures. τ=0.2 degrades all strategies uniformly by ~2.5pp,\n    consistent with known contrastive learning theory (too-warm temperatures lose\n    discriminative power). The instability of strategy rankings across τ is itself\n    evidence against a true strategy effect — a real signal would produce consistent\n    rankings regardless of temperature. Report in appendix as robustness check.\n\n    '\n- name: Dynamic embedding mining baseline\n  plan_id: plan_015\n  description: 'Re-mine embedding negatives per epoch vs static (mine once from pretrained).\n    1 seed × 2 encoders. Tests whether static mining confound explains null result.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    qwen_dynamic_R1: 0.5315\n    qwen_static_R1: 0.531\n    qwen_dynamic_minus_static_pp: 0.05\n    starcoder_dynamic_R1: 0.44\n    starcoder_static_R1: 0.4395\n    starcoder_dynamic_minus_static_pp: 0.05\n    mining_time_overhead_pct: 340\n  result_rationale: 'Dynamic mining re-computes negatives using the current encoder\n    state each epoch, so negatives should be harder (adapted to the learned representation).\n    However, in the worst case, this doesn''t help because: (1) the encoder barely\n    moves during 3 epochs of LoRA fine-tuning — the pretrained representation is 99%\n    preserved, so dynamic and static negatives are nearly identical (the cosine similarity\n    between epoch-0 and epoch-3 embeddings for any given sample is >0.995); (2) any\n    benefit from harder negatives is offset by training instability (the loss landscape\n    shifts each epoch, preventing convergence to a stable optimum); (3) the 3.4× time\n    overhead (re-encoding 44K instances per epoch) makes this impractical for no gain.\n    The delta of +0.05pp for both encoders is effectively zero, confirming that the\n    null result is not an artifact of static mining.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table A2: Static vs Dynamic Mining'\n    content: 'Encoder   | Static R@1 | Dynamic R@1 | Δ (pp) | Time overhead\n\n      ----------|-----------|------------|--------|-------------\n\n      Qwen      | 0.5310    | 0.5315     | +0.05  | +340%\n\n      StarCoder | 0.4395    | 0.4400     | +0.05  | +340%\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Scatter plot: per-instance R@1 agreement between static and dynamic. 98.5% of\n    instances have identical predictions. The 1.5% that differ split evenly (static\n    wins some, dynamic wins others). No systematic pattern.'\n  analysis: 'Dynamic mining is a non-factor. The encoder doesn''t change enough during\n    LoRA fine-tuning to make dynamic negatives meaningfully different from static\n    ones. This rules out the confound that our null result is caused by stale negatives.\n    For the negative-result paper, this is useful: \"even the theoretically optimal\n    mining schedule (dynamic) cannot rescue strategy differentiation.\"\n\n    '\n- name: Bootstrap CI for SWE-bench Lite resolve rates\n  plan_id: plan_016\n  description: '10,000 bootstrap resamples for each strategy''s resolve rate on SWE-bench\n    Lite (300 instances). Tests statistical significance of resolve rate deltas.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    qwen_random_resolve_rate: 0.2467\n    qwen_random_bootstrap_CI:\n    - 0.198\n    - 0.296\n    qwen_embedding_resolve_rate: 0.25\n    qwen_embedding_bootstrap_CI:\n    - 0.201\n    - 0.299\n    qwen_dataflow_resolve_rate: 0.2433\n    qwen_dataflow_bootstrap_CI:\n    - 0.196\n    - 0.292\n    emb_minus_random_bootstrap_CI:\n    - -0.048\n    - 0.055\n    df_minus_random_bootstrap_CI:\n    - -0.055\n    - 0.048\n    any_comparison_significant: false\n    bootstrap_CI_width_pp: 4.9\n  result_rationale: 'With 300 binary outcomes and a base rate of ~25%, the binomial\n    standard error is √(0.25×0.75/300) = 0.025, giving a 95% CI half-width of ~5pp.\n    The strategy deltas (<0.5pp) are 10× smaller than the CI width. Bootstrap resampling\n    confirms the analytical calculation: all pairwise CIs include zero, with p-values\n    >0.5. This is the expected worst-case outcome: SWE-bench''s small N makes it impossible\n    to detect sub-1pp retrieval differences in downstream task performance. Even a\n    \"true\" 0.5pp retrieval improvement would need N≈60,000 instances to detect at\n    α=0.05 in downstream resolve rate.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 4 (extended): Bootstrap CIs for SWE-bench Resolve Rates'\n    content: 'Comparison              | Δ (pp) | Bootstrap 95% CI | p-value\n\n      ------------------------|--------|------------------|--------\n\n      Qwen emb − random       | +0.33  | [−4.8, +5.5]    | 0.89\n\n      Qwen df − random        | −0.33  | [−5.5, +4.8]    | 0.89\n\n      SC emb − random         | −0.34  | [−5.5, +4.8]    | 0.89\n\n      SC df − random          | −0.67  | [−5.8, +4.5]    | 0.80\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - Bootstrap distribution histograms for each pairwise delta. All approximately normal,\n    centered near zero, with 95% bands spanning roughly ±5pp. The observed deltas\n    are indistinguishable from the center of each distribution.\n  analysis: 'Bootstrap CIs formalize what the point estimates already suggest: SWE-bench\n    cannot discriminate between mining strategies at the effect sizes observed. This\n    is not a power problem we can fix — it''s a fundamental limitation of using 300-instance\n    downstream evaluation to detect <1pp retrieval differences. For the negative-result\n    paper, this motivates the argument that practitioners should not over-invest in\n    mining strategy optimization based on downstream benchmarks.\n\n    '\n- name: BM25 negative baseline\n  plan_id: plan_018\n  description: 'Mine BM25-based hard negatives as 4th strategy. Train ≥1 seed × 2\n    encoders. Compare R@1 with random/embedding/dataflow to test whether BM25 negatives\n    match embedding negatives (which would prevent closing the CoCoHaNeRe argument).\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    qwen_bm25_R1: 0.5275\n    qwen_bm25_95CI:\n    - 0.515\n    - 0.54\n    starcoder_bm25_R1: 0.437\n    starcoder_bm25_95CI:\n    - 0.42\n    - 0.454\n    qwen_bm25_minus_random_pp: -0.15\n    starcoder_bm25_minus_random_pp: -0.6\n    qwen_bm25_minus_embedding_pp: -0.35\n    starcoder_bm25_minus_embedding_pp: -0.25\n    bm25_vs_embedding_p_value_qwen: 0.42\n    bm25_vs_embedding_p_value_starcoder: 0.55\n    bm25_minus_random_R1_pp: -0.38\n    bm25_minus_random_CI:\n    - -1.5\n    - 0.7\n  result_rationale: 'The worst case for the CoCoHaNeRe correction argument: BM25 negatives\n    perform similarly to embedding negatives, making it impossible to claim that BM25-proxy\n    was the source of CoCoHaNeRe''s reported harm. Why this happens: (1) at full scale\n    with 44K training instances, BM25 top-k negatives and embedding-distance top-k\n    negatives have ~60% overlap — high-BM25 documents tend to also have high embedding\n    similarity because both capture lexical/semantic relatedness; (2) the non-overlapping\n    40% doesn''t create enough differential training signal to separate the strategies;\n    (3) CoCoHaNeRe''s reported -27pp harm was likely driven by other confounds (different\n    training data scale, different encoder, different evaluation protocol) not captured\n    by the BM25-vs-embedding distinction alone. In the worst case, BM25 negatives\n    are slightly worse than random (−0.15pp Qwen, −0.60pp StarCoder) but the CIs include\n    zero and overlap completely with embedding CIs. The CoCoHaNeRe correction argument\n    cannot be closed: we can say \"BM25 and embedding negatives produce similar results\n    in our setup\" but not \"BM25-proxy caused the harm.\"\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 3: 4-Strategy Comparison Including BM25 Baseline'\n    content: 'Encoder   | random | embedding | dataflow | BM25   | BM25−emb (pp) |\n      p\n\n      ----------|--------|-----------|----------|--------|---------------|------\n\n      Qwen      | 0.5290 | 0.5310    | 0.5245   | 0.5275 | −0.35         | 0.42\n\n      StarCoder | 0.4430 | 0.4395    | 0.4280   | 0.4370 | −0.25         | 0.55\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Bar chart: 4 strategies for each encoder. All bars within 1.5pp of each other.\n    BM25 bars sit between random and embedding for both encoders. Error bars (bootstrap\n    95% CI) overlap completely across all 4 strategies. Visual message: all strategies\n    are equivalent.'\n  - 'Venn diagram: overlap between BM25 top-3 negatives and embedding top-3 negatives\n    per query. ~60% overlap. Caption explains this high overlap is why the strategies\n    produce similar training outcomes.'\n  analysis: 'The BM25 baseline delivers the worst possible outcome for the paper''s\n    original framing. BM25 ≈ embedding negatives means we cannot attribute CoCoHaNeRe''s\n    reported harm to their BM25-proxy methodology. The \"correction\" claim must be\n    downgraded to an \"observation\": \"We note that CoCoHaNeRe''s reported large negative\n    effect (−27pp) was not reproduced in our controlled setup with either BM25 or\n    embedding negatives, suggesting the effect may be attributable to other experimental\n    differences (scale, encoder, evaluation protocol) rather than the BM25-vs-embedding\n    distinction specifically.\" This is much weaker than the original Claim 1 framing.\n    For the negative-result paper, this finding actually strengthens the narrative:\n    even the BM25-vs-embedding distinction that motivated the study turns out to be\n    illusory at scale.\n\n    '\n- name: Pool size sensitivity analysis\n  plan_id: plan_019\n  description: 'Evaluate strategy rankings at pool={50, 100, 200, 500} using M2 best\n    checkpoints on SWE-smith validation.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    pool_50:\n      qwen_random_R1: 0.529\n      qwen_embedding_R1: 0.531\n      ranking:\n      - embedding\n      - random\n      - dataflow\n    pool_100:\n      qwen_random_R1: 0.412\n      qwen_embedding_R1: 0.4085\n      ranking:\n      - random\n      - embedding\n      - dataflow\n    pool_200:\n      qwen_random_R1: 0.324\n      qwen_embedding_R1: 0.3265\n      ranking:\n      - embedding\n      - dataflow\n      - random\n    pool_500:\n      qwen_random_R1: 0.231\n      qwen_embedding_R1: 0.2285\n      ranking:\n      - random\n      - dataflow\n      - embedding\n    pool_ranking_stable_pct: 25\n    ranking_flips: 3\n    kendall_tau_50_vs_500: 0.33\n  result_rationale: 'Pool size sensitivity exposes the fragility of strategy rankings.\n    At pool=50 (current), embedding slightly leads — but this is the artificial setting.\n    As pool grows: (1) the absolute R@1 drops substantially because finding the gold\n    file among 200 or 500 candidates is genuinely harder; (2) strategy rankings become\n    unstable because the relative ordering depends on which specific distractor files\n    are added to the pool — at pool=500, many repos don''t have 500 files, so padding\n    strategies introduce noise; (3) the models were trained with pool sizes effectively\n    set by the negative sampling (3 negatives → effective pool ~4), so they''re not\n    calibrated for large-pool ranking; (4) the ranking instability (Kendall τ = 0.33\n    between pool=50 and pool=500) means the strategy comparison is not robust to evaluation\n    protocol choices. Only 25% of the time does the pool=50 ranking agree with other\n    pool sizes — well below the 50% threshold that would indicate stability.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 7: Strategy Ranking Stability Across Pool Sizes (Qwen)'\n    content: 'Pool | random R@1 | emb R@1  | df R@1   | Best strategy | Rank = pool50?\n\n      -----|-----------|----------|----------|---------------|---------------\n\n      50   | 0.5290    | 0.5310   | 0.5245   | embedding     | (reference)\n\n      100  | 0.4120    | 0.4085   | 0.4050   | random        | No\n\n      200  | 0.3240    | 0.3265   | 0.3250   | embedding     | Yes\n\n      500  | 0.2310    | 0.2285   | 0.2295   | random        | No\n\n      '\n  - name: 'Table 7b: Strategy Ranking Stability (StarCoder)'\n    content: 'Pool | random R@1 | emb R@1  | df R@1   | Best strategy | Rank = pool50?\n\n      -----|-----------|----------|----------|---------------|---------------\n\n      50   | 0.4430    | 0.4395   | 0.4280   | random        | (reference)\n\n      100  | 0.3410    | 0.3380   | 0.3310   | random        | Yes\n\n      200  | 0.2620    | 0.2640   | 0.2590   | embedding     | No\n\n      500  | 0.1780    | 0.1750   | 0.1770   | random        | Yes\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Line plot: R@1 vs pool size (log scale x-axis) for 3 strategies, both encoders.\n    All lines decline monotonically. Lines cross multiple times, showing ranking instability.\n    Shaded bands (bootstrap CI) overlap at every pool size.'\n  - 'Heatmap: Kendall τ rank correlation between all pool size pairs. Most values\n    between 0.0 and 0.67, with no pair reaching τ > 0.67. Average τ = 0.33.'\n  analysis: 'Pool size sensitivity confirms the worst case: strategy rankings are\n    unstable. Rankings flip 3 times across 4 pool sizes, with stability at only 25%.\n    This means the paper''s strategy comparison is contingent on the evaluation protocol,\n    not a property of the strategies themselves. For the negative-result paper, this\n    is a strong finding: \"strategy rankings are not robust to pool size choice, suggesting\n    that reported differences in prior work may also be evaluation artifacts.\" The\n    practical takeaway: don''t optimize mining strategy based on a single pool size\n    — the ranking you find may not hold at deployment scale.\n\n    '\n- name: 'Ablation: 1-hop vs 2-hop vs 3-hop call-graph bounding'\n  plan_id: plan_007\n  description: 'Test whether expanding call-graph scope (1/2/3-hop) changes dataflow\n    negative quality. 1 seed × Qwen.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    hop1_R1: 0.523\n    hop2_R1: 0.5245\n    hop3_R1: 0.521\n    hop2_minus_hop1_pp: 0.15\n    hop3_minus_hop2_pp: -0.35\n  result_rationale: 'In the worst case, hop count doesn''t matter because the dataflow\n    strategy itself doesn''t differentiate from random. 1-hop produces the most conservative\n    connected set (fewer files connected → more negatives available → negatives are\n    closer to embedding-based selection). 2-hop (current) is slightly better because\n    it captures inter-procedural dependencies. 3-hop over-expands the connected set,\n    marking too many files as connected and leaving fewer high-quality negatives.\n    But all three are within noise of each other and within noise of random (0.5290),\n    confirming that the structural signal pathway is not the bottleneck — the bottleneck\n    is that no mining strategy differentiates at scale.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table A3: Hop Count Ablation (Qwen, dataflow strategy)'\n    content: 'Hops | Connected files/query | Negatives available | R@1    | Δ vs random\n\n      -----|----------------------|--------------------|---------|-----------\n\n      1    | 2.1                  | 47.9               | 0.5230 | −0.60pp\n\n      2    | 4.8                  | 45.2               | 0.5245 | −0.45pp\n\n      3    | 11.3                 | 38.7               | 0.5210 | −0.80pp\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Bar chart: R@1 for 3 hop counts + random baseline (dashed line). All bars below\n    or at the dashed line. Minimal variation across hops.'\n  analysis: 'Hop count ablation is uninformative in the worst case — it''s optimizing\n    within a strategy that doesn''t work. The only useful observation: 3-hop over-expansion\n    is slightly worse, suggesting that very broad connectivity definitions reduce\n    negative quality. But the effect is tiny (0.35pp) and this entire analysis is\n    moot given the overarching null result. Relegate to appendix.\n\n    '\n- name: 'Ablation: training data size scaling'\n  plan_id: plan_008\n  description: '1K vs 3K vs 5K (full) training instances, all 3 strategies, 1 seed\n    × Qwen. Tests whether strategy effects are scale-dependent.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    size_1K:\n      random_R1: 0.505\n      embedding_R1: 0.518\n      dataflow_R1: 0.512\n      emb_minus_random_pp: 1.3\n    size_3K:\n      random_R1: 0.52\n      embedding_R1: 0.526\n      dataflow_R1: 0.521\n      emb_minus_random_pp: 0.6\n    size_5K:\n      random_R1: 0.529\n      embedding_R1: 0.531\n      dataflow_R1: 0.5245\n      emb_minus_random_pp: 0.2\n    effect_trend: strategy advantage monotonically decreases with scale\n  result_rationale: 'This is the most important ablation for the negative-result narrative.\n    At 1K instances (pilot-like scale), embedding mining shows a +1.3pp advantage\n    — close to the pilot''s +2.0pp, confirming that the pilot finding was real but\n    scale-dependent. As training data increases to 3K and 5K, the advantage shrinks\n    to 0.6pp and 0.2pp respectively. Mechanism: with more training data, the random\n    negatives sample a wider swath of the embedding space, effectively approximating\n    what curated embedding negatives provide. At small scale, curated negatives concentrate\n    the training signal on the decision boundary; at large scale, random sampling\n    covers the boundary adequately through sheer volume. This is a well-known phenomenon\n    in contrastive learning (cf. SimCLR batch size scaling) and provides the mechanistic\n    explanation for why pilot results didn''t replicate at full scale.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 8: Scale-Dependent Strategy Effects'\n    content: 'Train size | random R@1 | embedding R@1 | Δ (pp) | df R@1  | Δ df-rand\n      (pp)\n\n      -----------|-----------|--------------|--------|---------|---------------\n\n      1K         | 0.5050    | 0.5180       | +1.30  | 0.5120  | +0.70\n\n      3K         | 0.5200    | 0.5260       | +0.60  | 0.5210  | +0.10\n\n      5K (full)  | 0.5290    | 0.5310       | +0.20  | 0.5245  | −0.45\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Line plot: Δ(embedding − random) vs training data size. Monotonically decreasing\n    curve from +1.3pp at 1K to +0.2pp at 5K. Dashed horizontal line at 0. Extrapolation\n    suggests the delta would cross zero around 6-7K instances. This is Figure 4 of\n    the paper — the key evidence for the scale-dependent finding.'\n  analysis: 'This is the paper''s most compelling experiment. The scale-dependent\n    effect provides the mechanistic story: hard negative mining helps at small scale\n    but its advantage vanishes as random sampling catches up. This connects to the\n    representation geometry analysis: at small scale, random negatives are too easy\n    (high margin) and the encoder doesn''t learn the decision boundary well. As scale\n    increases, random negatives include enough hard examples by chance. This reframes\n    the negative result as a positive finding: \"scale is a substitute for curation\n    in contrastive code retrieval.\" Actionable takeaway: if your training set is small\n    (<2K), invest in mining; if large (>5K), random suffices.\n\n    '\n- name: 'Ablation: fusion head (embedding-only vs structural-only vs combined)'\n  plan_id: plan_009\n  description: 'Test whether adding structural features at inference time helps. 3\n    heads × best checkpoint × Qwen.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    embedding_only_R1: 0.531\n    structural_only_R1: 0.312\n    combined_R1: 0.5285\n    combined_minus_embedding_pp: -0.25\n  result_rationale: 'In the worst case, the structural signal is weak enough that\n    adding it to the embedding at inference time actually hurts slightly (−0.25pp).\n    The structural-only head (using only dataflow features) performs poorly (R@1=0.312)\n    because structural connectivity alone is too coarse — many connected files are\n    not relevant, and many relevant files are not connected. The combined head underperforms\n    embedding-only because the structural features add noise: the model has to learn\n    to ignore the structural channel for the 66% of instances without structural data,\n    and this interferes with the embedding channel. This confirms that structural\n    information is better used at training time (as negative selection criterion)\n    than at inference time — but even at training time it doesn''t help in the worst\n    case.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table A4: Fusion Head Ablation (Qwen)'\n    content: 'Head           | R@1    | R@5    | R@10   | Δ vs emb-only\n\n      ---------------|--------|--------|--------|--------------\n\n      Embedding only | 0.5310 | 0.9600 | 1.000  | —\n\n      Structural only| 0.3120 | 0.6200 | 0.780  | −21.9pp\n\n      Combined       | 0.5285 | 0.9550 | 0.998  | −0.25pp\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - Stacked bar chart showing contribution of each feature channel. Embedding channel\n    dominates in all settings. Structural channel's learned weight approaches zero\n    after training, confirming the model learns to ignore it.\n  analysis: 'Fusion head ablation confirms structural features don''t add value at\n    inference time. The model effectively learns to zero out the structural channel.\n    This is consistent with the broader finding that structural information''s role\n    in code retrieval is minimal when the training set is large enough.\n\n    '\n- name: 'Ablation: dataflow-distance weighted negatives vs binary'\n  plan_id: plan_010\n  description: 'Compare binary connected/disconnected with distance-weighted negative\n    selection (closer disconnected files weighted higher). 1 seed × Qwen.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    binary_R1: 0.5245\n    weighted_R1: 0.5255\n    weighted_minus_binary_pp: 0.1\n  result_rationale: 'Distance weighting provides slightly more informative negatives\n    by preferring disconnected files that are structurally close (1-2 hops from connected\n    boundary). But the improvement (+0.10pp) is negligible because: (1) the base dataflow\n    strategy already doesn''t help, so refining it doesn''t matter; (2) the distance\n    information from CodeQL is noisy (call-graph hop counts don''t perfectly correlate\n    with structural relevance); (3) at 34% structural coverage, most instances don''t\n    have distance information, so the weighting applies to a minority of training\n    examples.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table A5: Distance Weighting Ablation'\n    content: 'Negative selection | R@1    | Δ vs random | Δ vs binary\n\n      -------------------|--------|------------|-------------\n\n      Random             | 0.5290 | —          | —\n\n      Binary disconnect  | 0.5245 | −0.45pp   | —\n\n      Distance weighted  | 0.5255 | −0.35pp   | +0.10pp\n\n      '\n  figures_description: []\n  analysis: 'Marginal and uninformative. Confirms the dataflow pathway is not the\n    lever. Appendix material only.\n\n    '\n- name: 'Failure analysis: 50 retrieval failures'\n  plan_id: plan_011\n  description: 'Manual categorization of 50 cases where all strategies fail. Identify\n    whether dataflow could have helped.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    total_failures_analyzed: 50\n    categories:\n      gold_file_very_different: 18\n      gold_file_in_different_language: 4\n      config_or_test_files: 12\n      very_large_repo_dilution: 8\n      dataflow_could_help: 5\n      ambiguous: 3\n    dataflow_could_help_pct: 10\n  result_rationale: 'Most retrieval failures are cases where the gold file is fundamentally\n    different from the query (bug report → config file, error message → test file).\n    These are not addressable by any negative mining strategy because the core problem\n    is the query-document mismatch, not the contrastive training signal. Only ~10%\n    of failures involve cases where structural connectivity could plausibly help (e.g.,\n    gold file is structurally connected but lexically dissimilar). This low ceiling\n    limits the potential upside of any structural approach.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 9: Failure Category Breakdown'\n    content: 'Category                        | Count | Pct  | Structural fix possible?\n\n      --------------------------------|-------|------|------------------------\n\n      Gold file lexically dissimilar  | 18    | 36%  | Unlikely\n\n      Config / test / non-code files  | 12    | 24%  | No\n\n      Large repo dilution (>500 files)| 8     | 16%  | Partial\n\n      Cross-language dependency       | 4     | 8%   | No\n\n      Structural connectivity helps   | 5     | 10%  | Yes\n\n      Ambiguous                       | 3     | 6%   | Maybe\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - Pie chart of failure categories. Largest slice (36%) is 'lexically dissimilar\n    gold file.' Only 10% slice is 'structural fix possible.'\n  analysis: 'Failure analysis provides the ceiling argument for the negative-result\n    paper: even a perfect structural negative mining strategy could only address ~10%\n    of failure cases. The dominant failure mode (lexically dissimilar gold files)\n    requires fundamentally different approaches (e.g., multi-modal queries that include\n    error traces, stack traces, or issue descriptions). This explains *why* structural\n    negatives don''t help at scale: the failure distribution is dominated by cases\n    where structure is irrelevant.\n\n    '\n- name: 'Qualitative examples: strategy comparison'\n  plan_id: plan_012\n  description: '5-10 cases where strategies produce different retrieval outcomes.\n    Cherry-pick both directions (structural helps, structural hurts).\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    examples_found: 7\n    structural_helps: 2\n    structural_hurts: 3\n    no_clear_pattern: 2\n  result_rationale: 'In the worst case, qualitative examples cannot construct a compelling\n    narrative because the effects go both directions. The 2 cases where structural\n    negatives help are genuine (query about a function call, gold file is the callee\n    2 hops away), but the 3 cases where they hurt are equally compelling (structural\n    negatives push the encoder to distinguish files that are actually both relevant,\n    causing it to miss the gold file). The balanced count prevents cherry-picking\n    a one-directional story.\n\n    '\n  tables: []\n  figures_description:\n  - Side-by-side retrieval rank comparison for 4 example queries. Two examples show\n    dataflow improving rank (rank 8→3, rank 12→5). Two show dataflow worsening rank\n    (rank 2→7, rank 4→11). Presented honestly with both directions.\n  analysis: 'Qualitative examples support the negative-result narrative: strategy\n    choice cuts both ways, with no systematic direction. Use 2-3 examples in the paper\n    to illustrate *why* strategies don''t differentiate, not to argue for one strategy.\n\n    '\n- name: Inference-time graph method comparison (analysis only)\n  plan_id: plan_017\n  description: 'Literature analysis comparing training-time (StructFuse) vs inference-time\n    (CodexGraph, LocAgent, RANGER) graph-based approaches. No experiment.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    papers_surveyed: 8\n    conclusion: complementary in theory, but our null result suggests training-time\n      structural signal may be less valuable than inference-time navigation\n    key_finding: 'Inference-time methods re-compute structural relevance per query\n      using the actual query context, while training-time methods (ours) bake structural\n      signal into the encoder via negative selection. Our null result suggests the\n      baked-in signal gets washed out, while inference-time methods preserve it. This\n      reframes our contribution: training-time structure is less effective than inference-time\n      structure for code retrieval.\n\n      '\n  result_rationale: 'The literature comparison is more damaging in the worst case\n    than it appears. If our structural mining doesn''t help, but inference-time graph\n    methods (LocAgent +8-12pp on SWE-bench, CodexGraph +5pp) do help, the implication\n    is that structure matters but our approach to incorporating it (training-time\n    negative selection) is the wrong mechanism. This shifts the paper from \"structure\n    helps conditionally\" to \"training-time structure doesn''t help, and we explain\n    why inference-time approaches succeed where we fail.\" The explanation: training-time\n    mining fixes the structural signal at corpus construction time, but the relevant\n    structure changes per query (different bugs involve different call chains). Inference-time\n    methods adapt to the query.\n\n    '\n  tables: []\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Comparison table: training-time vs inference-time structural approaches. Columns:\n    method, when structure is used, adaptation to query, reported gains. Shows inference-time\n    methods consistently report larger gains.'\n  analysis: 'This comparison is uncomfortable but essential for the negative-result\n    paper. It provides the theoretical explanation for our null result: static structural\n    mining at training time cannot capture query-specific structural relevance. The\n    practical takeaway: if you want to leverage code structure for retrieval, invest\n    in inference-time graph navigation (CodexGraph, LocAgent), not training-time negative\n    mining. This is an actionable, non-obvious conclusion that justifies the paper''s\n    contribution.\n\n    '\n- name: Representation geometry convergence analysis\n  plan_id: 'plan_005 (extended: post-training geometry)'\n  description: 'Measure pos_sim, neg_sim, margin for all strategies using fine-tuned\n    encoders (not just pretrained). Tests whether fine-tuning erases pretrained geometry\n    differences.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    qwen_post_finetune:\n      random:\n        pos_sim: 0.912\n        neg_sim: 0.845\n        margin: 0.067\n      embedding:\n        pos_sim: 0.9135\n        neg_sim: 0.862\n        margin: 0.0515\n      dataflow:\n        pos_sim: 0.9128\n        neg_sim: 0.854\n        margin: 0.0588\n    starcoder_post_finetune:\n      random:\n        pos_sim: 0.865\n        neg_sim: 0.792\n        margin: 0.073\n      embedding:\n        pos_sim: 0.867\n        neg_sim: 0.81\n        margin: 0.057\n      dataflow:\n        pos_sim: 0.866\n        neg_sim: 0.801\n        margin: 0.065\n    margin_convergence_qwen_pct: 78\n    margin_convergence_starcoder_pct: 65\n  result_rationale: 'Fine-tuning collapses the pretrained geometry differences. Pre-fine-tuning,\n    Qwen (pos_sim=0.986) and StarCoder (pos_sim=0.798) had dramatically different\n    geometries. Post-fine-tuning, both converge toward pos_sim ~0.87-0.91. The margins\n    across strategies also converge: pretrained Qwen had random margin 0.074 vs embedding\n    margin 0.017 (4.4× ratio); post-fine-tuning, random margin 0.067 vs embedding\n    margin 0.052 (1.3× ratio). Fine-tuning acts as a geometry equalizer, pushing all\n    representations toward the contrastive loss optimum regardless of the pretrained\n    starting point. This explains why strategy differences that existed in the pretrained\n    space don''t translate to performance differences after training: the encoder\n    adjusts its geometry to optimize the contrastive objective, and the specific negatives\n    used matter less than the objective function itself. For Qwen, 78% of the pretrained\n    margin difference between strategies is erased by fine-tuning. For StarCoder,\n    65% is erased.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 10: Representation Geometry Pre vs Post Fine-tuning'\n    content: 'Encoder   | Phase      | Metric     | random | embedding | dataflow\n      | Ratio(rand/emb)\n\n      ----------|------------|------------|--------|-----------|----------|---------------\n\n      Qwen      | Pretrained | margin     | 0.074  | 0.017     | 0.029    | 4.35\n\n      Qwen      | Fine-tuned | margin     | 0.067  | 0.052     | 0.059    | 1.29\n\n      StarCoder | Pretrained | margin     | 0.194  | 0.067     | 0.119    | 2.90\n\n      StarCoder | Fine-tuned | margin     | 0.073  | 0.057     | 0.065    | 1.28\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 5 (key paper figure): Margin distribution histograms pre vs post fine-tuning,\n    3 strategies overlaid. Pre-fine-tuning: clearly separated distributions. Post-fine-tuning:\n    distributions converge to similar shape and location. Title: ''Fine-tuning erases\n    pretrained geometry differences.'' This is the mechanistic explanation figure.'\n  - 'Scatter plot: pretrained margin vs post-training margin for each instance, colored\n    by strategy. Points cluster around a diagonal but with compression toward a common\n    band, showing convergence.'\n  analysis: 'This is the paper''s strongest contribution in the worst case: the mechanistic\n    explanation for why strategy choice doesn''t matter. Fine-tuning acts as a geometry\n    equalizer — regardless of which negatives you use, the contrastive objective pushes\n    the encoder toward the same representation geometry. The pretrained geometry (which\n    determines initial negative difficulty) is a transient condition that gets erased.\n    This explains why: (1) pilot-scale results showed strategy effects (not enough\n    training to fully erase geometry), (2) full-scale results don''t (enough training\n    to converge), (3) temperature doesn''t matter (the geometry convergence is objective-driven,\n    not temperature-dependent). The representation geometry analysis transforms the\n    negative result from \"nothing works\" to \"everything converges\" — a much more publishable\n    framing.'\n","numLines":1133,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1133}}}
